libraries.documentation:
/// The behaviour every [TomJobStore] and every [TomLeaseLock] must exhibit,
/// written once and run against every implementation.
///
/// ## Why this file exists, and why it lives in `test/`
///
/// The kernel ships two implementations of each port (memory and file); the
/// relational pair lives one layer up in `tom_core_server`, over the same
/// contract. A second implementation that merely *resembles* the first is not
/// good enough — this very suite caught a file-lock divergence in which a live
/// holder re-acquiring its own lease was issued a *new* fencing token, silently
/// fencing itself out. So the suite is the shared artefact and
/// each implementation is a parameter.
///
/// It sits in `test/` rather than `lib/` deliberately. Putting it in `lib/`
/// would make `package:test` a *regular* dependency of `tom_core_kernel`, and
/// therefore of every consumer — including Flutter applications, which reach
/// the kernel through `tom_core_flutter` and whose `flutter_test` pins its own
/// `test_api` / `matcher`. Injecting `package:test` into that graph to serve a
/// test-only need is not a trade worth making. The alternatives were weighed
/// and rejected in the same pass:
///
///  * a separate `tom_core_scheduling_contract` package — the idiomatic Dart
///    answer, but it costs a pubspec, a `tom_master.yaml` registration and a
///    build-order entry to carry two functions;
///  * adding the SQL variants to the kernel's own parameterised loop — the DB
///    fixture (`tom_core_server/test/fixtures/test_database.dart`) is not
///    importable from the kernel, and the dependency direction would be wrong
///    anyway.
///
/// What remains is a relative import from the consuming package's test —
/// `import '../../tom_core_kernel/test/contracts/scheduling_contract.dart';`.
/// It is unusual, and it only works because the two packages sit side by side
/// in this monorepo, which they do by construction (`tom_ai/core/`).
///
/// ## What a portable contract may assert
///
/// The in-memory store hands back the *same objects* it was given, so the
/// kernel's own tests could assert on a caller-held run after a store call. A
/// relational store builds fresh objects from rows and can only mutate the
/// instances it is actually passed. The abstract class never promised object
/// identity, so this suite asserts against re-read state (`all()`,
/// `findWindow()`) wherever the distinction could bite. That is a narrowing of
/// the *tests*, not of the contract.

classes.documentation:
/// A clock the test moves by hand.
///
/// Lease expiry is absolute-time arithmetic, so every lock implementation —
/// including the relational one, which computes `now` in Dart and stores the
/// resulting instant — can be driven by this without waiting on wall time.

functions.documentation:
/// Declares the [TomJobStore] contract as a group named after [variant].
///
/// [create] must return a store with no runs in it; a durable implementation is
/// expected to clear its backing table or file first.

/// Declares the [TomLeaseLock] contract as a group named after [variant].
///
/// [create] must return a lock over a resource namespace with no live leases in
/// it, driven by the supplied clock.

/// What the timezoned module's conditional import selects, whether the
/// selected file can run where it is selected, and whether the documentation
/// says the same thing.
///
/// `timezones.dart` picks its initializer with a conditional import:
///
/// ```dart
/// import 'init_tz_embedded.dart' if (dart.library.js) 'init_tz_web.dart';
/// ```
///
/// The first entry is the **default** arm, taken wherever no condition holds.
/// That is the position with the strongest requirement in the whole construct
/// and the easiest to misjudge: an arm reached where nothing can be assumed
/// must assume nothing, so it may not touch `dart:io` any more than it may
/// touch `dart:js`. The embedded database qualifies — it is a constant and a
/// decoder — and it is the default for exactly that reason.
///
/// Both mistakes this file guards against were invisible from one side alone.
/// A default arm that pulled in `dart:io` looked fine in isolation, because
/// nothing in the file names the condition that would exclude it, and it
/// compiled everywhere anyone ever built. And prose that credited the wrong
/// platform with the wrong entry point looked fine too, because the source is
/// correct and says nothing about platforms while the prose names platforms
/// and quotes no condition. So the tests hold the pieces together:
///
/// - the `import` and `export` arms stay identical, which the source asks for
///   in a comment and nothing else enforces;
/// - each arm's file says which selection it serves;
/// - no arm reaches a `dart:` library its own condition rules out, following
///   the imports through the `timezone` package to find out;
/// - the documentation quotes the block as written;
/// - every `package:timezone/…` library the documentation names is one the
///   module imports;
/// - every "*this entry point on that platform*" claim names a library the
///   platform's own arm actually reaches.

/// How a quotation of the conditional import begins, derived from the source
/// so that renaming the default arm cannot leave the scans looking for a file
/// that no longer exists — and silently finding nothing.

/// The `dart:` libraries an arm selected by `condition` cannot rely on.
///
/// The default arm is the strict case: it is chosen precisely where none of
/// the conditions hold, so *every* platform library is out of reach. The
/// conditional arms may use what their own condition guarantees and nothing
/// from the other side.

/// Reads `fileName` and everything it reaches inside `package:timezone`.
///
/// The walk stops at every other package on purpose. What this conditional
/// import chooses between is `timezone` entry points, and `timezone` is the
/// dependency whose platform surface differs across them — descending into an
/// unrelated package would only report that package's own conditional imports,
/// which it resolves for itself.

/// The condition that selects the arm serving a platform, or `null` for the
/// default arm.
///
/// Native and Flutter are the same answer on purpose, and that answer is the
/// default arm: neither is singled out by a condition, so both are served by
/// whatever serves everything else. Giving them separate rows is what produced
/// the wrong table.

variables.documentation:
/// The word each conditional arm's dartdoc has to contain to have described
/// where it runs. The default arm is not here — it is checked separately,
/// because what it has to say is about its position, not about a platform.

functions.documentation:
/// Every artefact this package ships must read as a statement of what the code
/// does *now*.
///
/// Two habits break that, and both are easy to reintroduce because both feel
/// helpful while the work is fresh:
///
/// 1. **Work-item pointers** — `bug §50`, `tests §34a`, `tcinc262`. They
///    resolve only against quest documents, which nobody reading the published
///    package has. To that reader they are noise that looks like it means
///    something.
/// 2. **History narration** — "used to be", "before the fix". A reader needs
///    the *rule*, not the changelog: "a legitimate domain scope whose local id
///    happens to be 'default' is not the sentinel" carries the whole reason,
///    while "before the fix it was treated as the sentinel" makes them
///    reconstruct it.
///
/// Traceability still exists — it just points the other way. A todo names the
/// files it touched in its own `references` list, so the link survives without
/// the code carrying an identifier that outlives the work item.
///
/// This is a test rather than a shell script on purpose: it runs on every
/// `testkit :test`, so a regression surfaces in the ordinary test run.
///
/// **It covers this package only, and it is not the rule.**
/// `tool/check_comments_read_as_is.py` and `tool/check_section_refs.py` apply
/// the same two habits to every package in the repository and are the
/// authority; this test is the fast half of the same convention. Keeping the
/// coverage in one repo-wide gate rather than in a copy of this file per
/// package is a decision the existing copies earned: a fix to the id rule was
/// once made in the `advanced_ops_server` copy and never travelled back here.
///
/// What made that possible was that each copy carried its own *vocabulary* —
/// a list of quest prefixes, which every copy had to be told about separately.
/// None does now. The rule is a shape plus a list of English words, so the
/// only thing a copy can hold that its siblings do not is which words that
/// package's own examples use.

/// A quest todo id, or a section pointer into a quest's bug/test lists
/// (`bug §50`, `tests §34a`, a bare `§45`).
///
/// The bare-`§` case is the interesting one, because three *legitimate* uses of
/// the character have to survive it — see [_allowedSectionRefs].

/// Files exempt from the scan, and why each one is.
///
/// - This file states the patterns it forbids, so it matches all of them.
/// - `CHANGELOG.md` is a history document by definition; naming the work item
///   behind an entry is what a changelog is *for*. It sits in [_scannedRoot],
///   so this exemption is what actually keeps it out.
/// - `doc/analyzer_analysis.json` is generated: it snapshots dartdoc text, so
///   it carries whatever the sources carried at generation time and is fixed by
///   regenerating, never by editing.

variables.documentation:
/// Stems that take a numeric suffix in ordinary prose *in this package*.
/// Anything matching [_idShape] whose stem is not here is a work-item pointer.
///
/// **This list is English, not quest vocabulary, and that is what makes it
/// safe.** The rule used to enumerate quest prefixes — `tcinc`, `tcca`, `tccc`
/// and five more. That is an *open* set: it grows whenever anyone mints a
/// prefix, and until someone adds the new one the guard reports the package
/// clean. It failed exactly that way; `qrc1` and `tfca4` were shipping in
/// `tom_core_server` dartdoc while every copy of this guard passed.
///
/// Enumerating words instead closes the set — minting `zzq7` does not make
/// `zzq` a word — so the failure direction inverts with it: the prefix list
/// failed open and silently, this fails closed and loudly. A new worked
/// example naming `widget1` costs one entry here, which is the cheap half.
///
/// **Why the per-package copies may differ here and nowhere else.** These are
/// the words *this* package's examples happen to use. There is no quest
/// vocabulary left in any copy, so the copies cannot drift apart on the rule —
/// only on which English words they need, which is a fact about each package.

/// The same shape in capitals — `TCCA17`, `CSEXF1`, `RCK13`, `OT2`.
///
/// Case is a bypass: [_idShape] is lowercase, so an id written the way prose
/// usually writes one — shouted — walks straight past it.
///
/// Capitals are matched only as a *whole run*, never camelCase, and that is
/// what keeps [_knownCaps] short. A Dart identifier with a numeric suffix is
/// written `addressLine1`, `promptSha256`, `mBlue100`; a work-item id never is.

/// The two shapes an id is written in, each with its own vocabulary. Kept
/// apart so neither list has to carry the other's words: exempting `SHA` on
/// the lowercase side would exempt `sha` for free, and vice versa.

/// The parts of a line a reader reads as prose, which is the only place a
/// pointer at a work item can sit. In Dart that is comments and the
/// descriptions of tests and groups; in markdown and shell it is the line.
///
/// Not every string literal: widening it that far drowns the rule in fixture
/// data (`user1`, `task2`), which is data the test feeds to the code rather
/// than prose a reader is asked to understand.

/// Phrasings that describe a previous state of the code rather than the rule.
///
/// Deliberately narrow. "used to" also means "used in order to" ("an Exception
/// used to fail a command"), and "was removed"/"has since been" routinely
/// describe *runtime* events ("returns true when an entry was removed"), so
/// those bare forms are excluded — a guard that cries wolf gets switched off.
/// The verb list is the discriminator: each of these reads as a claim about
/// the past and none of them follows "used in order to", which is why
/// `resolve`, `reach`, `look` and `present` are absent however often they
/// narrate — demanding a rewrite of "the scope segment used to resolve display
/// keys" would be the crying wolf.
///
/// `as before` and `formerly` have no innocent reading in shipped text:
/// whatever they compare to, the reader is not holding it. `Previously`
/// leading a sentence is narration; the same word inside one is usually
/// adjectival ("previously persisted records"), so the lowercase form is
/// matched only where a determiner or pronoun follows and a clause is
/// therefore starting.
///
/// Kept in step with `tool/check_comments_read_as_is.py`, which enforces the
/// same rule across every package in `tom_ai/core`.

/// The `§` uses that are *not* work-item pointers and must not be flagged.
///
/// A section number is allowed exactly when the line says what it is a section
/// *of*, because that is the whole difference between a citation and a dangling
/// number. The named thing has to be something the reader can reach:
///
/// - a published standard — `RFC 4226 §5.3`, `CWE-117`;
/// - a specification this package implements — `CE-ER §7`,
///   `codespecs_mapping.md §5.26`;
/// - a sibling document — `doc/logging/logging.md § "What the collector does"`.
///
/// The naming is required on the same line, not merely somewhere in the file.
/// Dartdoc is read one member at a time — in the generated API docs a member's
/// paragraph appears with nothing around it — so a document named forty lines
/// up is not available to the person reading the citation. The practical
/// consequence is that a citation may not be broken across a wrap: reflow the
/// paragraph so the document and its section number stay on one line.
///
/// There is deliberately no exemption for the *shape* of the number. Allowing
/// `§<digits>.<digits>` on the grounds that quest bug lists are flat tests
/// where a number came from rather than whether a reader can follow it, and it
/// let three unattributed `§5.26` citations sit in `access_controls.dart` —
/// real spec sections, unreachable all the same.

/// Trees whose contents a package consumer can read, scanned recursively.
///
/// `tool/` is here for its shell scripts: a `bug §52` in a comment is exactly
/// as unresolvable to the reader of a script as to the reader of a library.

/// The package root, scanned **non-recursively** for the documents that sit
/// beside `pubspec.yaml` — `README.md` above all, which is the pub.dev landing
/// page and so the most-read artefact the package has.
///
/// Non-recursive on purpose. A recursive root scan would pull in
/// `_copilot_guidelines/` — development docs, where naming the work item is
/// the correct thing to do — along with build and log directories.

/// A testkit id in the leading `ID: description` position of a test or group
/// name (`tccc3-05: reports at ERROR level`). That position becomes the ID
/// column of `testlog/baseline_*.csv` — an identifier in a live tracking
/// system, not a pointer into a finished work item — and renaming one discards
/// that test's whole result history.
///
/// Only the leading position. The same id in prose is a pointer like any
/// other; exempting it anywhere on the line, as this guard once did, let a
/// work-item pointer ride along on any line that also opened a test.

libraries.documentation:
/// The guard that keeps the timezone setup functions reachable from the
/// package barrel.
///
/// `tomSetupTimezones` is not a convenience. On web the database is fetched
/// over the network, there is no synchronous load path, and `TomTimezone`
/// throws on every access until the fetch has completed — so an application
/// that cannot call this function cannot use the module at all. Four documents
/// instruct web startup to call it.
///
/// It was nonetheless unreachable: `timezones.dart` pulled the
/// platform-conditional implementation in with an `import`, which brings names
/// into the importing library without passing them on, so nothing crossed the
/// barrel. Native callers never noticed, because there the synchronous
/// lazy-init makes the call optional; the break was invisible everywhere except
/// the one platform where the call is mandatory.
///
/// That is the shape worth guarding: a public symbol whose absence only shows
/// up on a platform the test suite does not run on. So the check is made
/// compile-time rather than conditional — this file imports **nothing but the
/// barrel** and names both functions, so removing the re-export does not
/// produce a failing assertion, it produces a file that will not compile, on
/// every platform, immediately.
///
/// The first test exists to keep that property true. The guard's entire
/// strength is the import list, and an import list is exactly the kind of thing
/// a later edit widens without meaning anything by it: add one relative import
/// into `lib/src/`, and this file still passes while proving nothing. So it
/// reads its own source and asserts the import list is still just the barrel.

/// Unit tests for the kernel's pluggable refresh-once-on-expiry coordinator
/// [tomRunWithAuthRetry]: it must recover a single expired-token failure by
/// refreshing and replaying exactly once, give up (as a typed error) on a
/// persistent expiry without looping, leave non-expiry statuses untouched, and
/// honour the active [TomAuthRetryPolicy].
///
/// A tiny [_FakeRefresher] stands in for a live auth server (a "mock auth
/// server" at the coordinator seam), and a scripted response queue drives the
/// `send` callback — so the whole mechanism is exercised without a socket.

/// Tests for the scheduler runtime and durable job queue.
///
/// The suite is organised the way the module is: schedules are pure functions
/// and are tested by evaluation against fixed dates; the store is tested by
/// driving its state machine directly; the scheduler is tested by calling
/// [TomScheduler.tick] against an injected clock. Nothing here waits on wall
/// time except the two tests that deliberately exercise a real isolate pool and
/// a real periodic timer.

classes.documentation:
/// A command that blocks until the test releases it.
///
/// Lets a test hold a run "in flight" across an arbitrary number of awaits —
/// which is what the lease-renewal and lease-loss paths need, since both only
/// happen while work is genuinely outstanding.

/// A lock whose renewals stop landing after [beatsBeforeStall].
///
/// A node whose event loop stalls — a stop-the-world pause, a suspended VM, a
/// wedged disk — misses its heartbeats, and its lease lapses underneath it
/// while it still believes it holds it. That is the one way a live holder
/// genuinely loses a lease, and it cannot be produced by waiting: a healthy
/// heartbeat keeps a lease alive indefinitely, which is the whole point of
/// renewal. Failing the renewal reproduces the stall's only observable effect.

functions.documentation:
/// Lets the runs a `tick()` started actually finish.
///
/// `tick()` deliberately does not await the work it dispatches — a slow job must
/// not hold up the next window of a fast one — so completion, the store write
/// and the lease release all land in later turns of the event loop. Tests that
/// assert on the *aftermath* of a run have to yield first.
///
/// [pumpEventQueue] rather than a counted loop of zero delays: the number of
/// turns a dispatch takes is an implementation detail, and a test that encodes
/// it fails the next time the runtime gains an await — which is exactly what
/// adding the `markStarted` write did.

/// An event source for a [TomEventSchedule], closed at the end of the test.
///
/// Broadcast rather than single-subscription: `close()` on an unlistened
/// single-subscription controller never completes, and these tests hand the
/// controller to `addTearDown`, which awaits it — including in the cases where
/// the scheduler deliberately never subscribes or has already cancelled.

/// The dependency table in the timezoned prose, held to the module's imports.
///
/// A dependency table is the one part of a document that is wholly derivable
/// from the source, and it is also the part that goes stale most quietly. An
/// import added to a file changes nothing a reader can see; the table keeps
/// listing what the module used to need, and the omission looks exactly like a
/// dependency that was never there. The table had lost a `little_things` file
/// that way — the module reaches that module through two files and the table
/// named one.
///
/// So the source is the anchor and the table is compared to it in both
/// directions: a dependency with no row is an omission, a row with no
/// dependency is a leftover, and both are wrong in the same way. The
/// `Imported by` column is checked the same way, per dependency, because a
/// table can name the right four things and still attribute them to the wrong
/// files.
///
/// What this file does *not* cover is the conditional import that selects the
/// platform initializer, and the `package:timezone/…` entry points the prose
/// credits to each platform — `timezoned_platform_selection_test.dart` owns
/// those, following the imports into the `timezone` package to do it. Here a
/// dependency is a name and a set of importing files, nothing deeper.

/// Every dependency the module reaches outside its own directory, mapped to
/// the module files that import it.
///
/// A dependency is named the way the table names it: a package by its package
/// name, a sibling kernel module by its directory. Imports within the module
/// are not dependencies — they are the module — and neither are `dart:`
/// libraries, which no table row could usefully describe.

/// The sibling kernel files each sibling module is reached through, keyed by
/// module directory — `little_things` → `exception_base.dart`, `formatting.dart`.
///
/// Which files these are is the detail the table lost, so it is anchored
/// separately from the module name itself.

/// The table's rows, keyed by the dependency each one names.
///
/// The key comes out of the first cell, which names a package in backticks or
/// a sibling module as a link to that module's document. Deriving the key from
/// the link target rather than the link text is deliberate: the text is prose
/// ("Little Things Module") and the target is a path, and only the path can be
/// compared to an import.

/// The files a row's `Imported by` cell credits.
///
/// `every file` is the one phrase that names a set rather than its members, so
/// it is resolved against the module rather than parsed.

/// What the note claims that file imports.
///
/// Only what follows `imports only`, and only to the end of that sentence,
/// counts. Both bounds were needed. Before the sentence, the note names the
/// types that live in the file, in an aside, and those are backticked exactly
/// like the imports are — reading from the start would take three class names
/// for three dependencies. After it, the paragraph goes on to mention
/// `timezones.dart` again, so a scan running to the blank line would keep
/// finding that name however badly the claim itself was worded.

libraries.documentation:
/// The guard that keeps the package `dart format`-clean.
///
/// Formatting drift is invisible to the gate everybody actually runs.
/// `dart analyze` reports nothing about line wrapping, so a file can be
/// analyzer-clean, test-green, reviewed and merged while being unformatted —
/// and nothing says so until somebody runs the formatter and watches it rewrite
/// files they did not touch.
///
/// This package has already paid that bill. 83 % of it was unformatted — 97 of
/// 117 example files and 34 of 56 under `lib/` — which is what made the
/// reformat impossible to do in passing: a change that ran the formatter as a
/// side effect arrived with its substantive edit buried among a hundred
/// reflowed files, so the formatter got avoided, so the drift grew. It took two
/// wholesale commits to clear, one for `example/` and `lib/` and one for
/// `test/`.
///
/// Eighteen days later it drifted again, in six example files, all of them
/// rewritten under todos whose stated quality gate was `dart analyze` — which
/// by construction could not see it. That second drift is the reason this file
/// exists: the state had been restored by hand and was being held by hand, and
/// the first agent to touch the package undid part of it without being told.
///
/// The fix when this fails is always the same and never a code change: run
/// `dart format .`.
///
/// The scope is **every Dart file in the package**, derived by walking it, and
/// not a list of directories to keep up to date. A checker told where to look
/// stops covering the package the moment a file appears somewhere else, and
/// reports nothing while it does. Only two things are left out, each for a
/// stated reason: the dot-directories the package does not author, and
/// generated output.
///
/// **If this fails on a machine where nothing was edited**, the SDK's formatter
/// has changed its output. Reformat the package and commit that as its own
/// whitespace-only change. Do not relax this test to accommodate it: a
/// formatter change that nobody reformats for is how the first drift
/// accumulated.
///
/// The shape is `tom_core_server/test/formatting_test.dart`, which solved the
/// same problem in the sibling package first. Kept deliberately identical, down
/// to which files are excluded, so that the two packages cannot answer the same
/// question differently.

functions.documentation:
/// Machine-written files, which this package does not author and cannot fix.
///
/// A generator that emits unformatted code is the generator's defect, and
/// failing this suite for it would point the reader at the wrong package.
///
/// `version.versioner.dart` is generated too but is *not* excluded, matching
/// the sibling guard: its generator emits formatted output, so holding it to
/// the same rule costs nothing and would report a real regression if that
/// changed.

/// Every Dart file in the package, generated ones included, as paths relative
/// to the package root.
///
/// Derived by walking the package rather than from a list of directories, so a
/// script added under `tool/`, or a `build.dart` at the root, is guarded the
/// day it appears rather than the day someone remembers to widen this.
///
/// Dot-directories are skipped rather than filtered afterwards: `.dart_tool`
/// holds package copies this package does not author (and which the formatter
/// excludes anyway) and `.git` holds no Dart at all, so descending into either
/// would cost far more than the walk. Links are not followed, for the same
/// reason plus the loop they can form.

variables.documentation:
/// The directories a walk of this package must reach, whatever else it finds.
///
/// These are not the *scope* — the scope is every Dart file in the package (see
/// [_packageDartFiles]). They are landmarks: three directories that are
/// structural to the package and cannot legitimately become empty, so a walk
/// that misses one has gone wrong rather than found a smaller package.

libraries.documentation:
/// Every module readme names the whole surface its module exports.
///
/// A module readme opens with a **Key types** table, and a reader takes that
/// table for the module's reference surface. It is worth keeping true and it
/// does not stay true by itself: adding a type to a module is a one-line edit
/// away from leaving the table behind, and nothing about the omission is
/// visible from either side alone. Six of the kernel's modules had drifted at
/// once — `security` was missing 23 of 47 names, `observable` 14 of 33 — and
/// the drift was found by a hand-written scan rather than by anything standing.
///
/// The surface is read from the **package barrel**, not from the module
/// directory. A file that exists but is not exported is not public, and
/// demanding a readme document it would be wrong; conversely the barrel is what
/// a consumer actually sees. `lib/tom_core_kernel.dart` exports
/// `src/tombase/<module>/<file>.dart` a file at a time, so grouping those by
/// module gives each module's public files exactly.
///
/// A name counts only when it appears **as a whole word inside an inline code
/// span**, which is how these tables write API. A plain substring search passes
/// vacuously — and an abbreviation is what hid part of this drift in the first
/// place: `observable`'s table read `TomNString / TomNInt / TomND…`, which
/// names two of five nullable cells and leaves a reader unable to tell whether
/// the one they want exists. The ellipsis satisfied a reader and no check.

variables.documentation:
/// Modules the package barrel exports that ship no `readme.md`.
///
/// Empty, and the second test below keeps it that way in both directions: a
/// module that loses its readme has to be named here with a reason, and one
/// that gains a readme has to be removed. An entry is therefore always a live
/// admission rather than a stale exemption nobody revisits.

classes.documentation:
/// A mock implementation of [TomPlatformUtils] for testing.
///
/// This implementation allows configuring all platform detection methods
/// and provides working implementations for all abstract methods.

functions.documentation:
/// A group's `00_run_all_examples.dart` runs its samples back to back into one
/// continuous transcript, so a reader mapping a passage of it back to a source
/// file has only the ordinal to do it with.
///
/// Exactly one thing emits that ordinal: **the file's own banner**,
/// `=== 03: Shutdown Execution Example ===`, printed as the first thing `run()`
/// does. Renumbering a directory renames the files and does not touch the
/// string, so it drifts silently — the suite still passes and the transcript
/// simply points at the wrong source. Roughly a dozen files have carried a
/// stale or absent ordinal at one time or another, which is what makes this
/// worth a test rather than a periodic sweep.
///
/// "Exactly one" is itself a rule here, and a recent one. Group runners used to
/// print a label of their own before each call, so a file was named twice per
/// run by two strings written months apart; ten runners carried one and five
/// had drifted out of step with the sample they introduced, emitting two
/// different ordinals for one file in a single transcript. The labels are gone,
/// and a test below keeps them gone: the sample names itself, the runner names
/// the group, and there is one place to get the number wrong instead of two.
///
/// The complementary property — that the banner actually *reaches* the
/// transcript, rather than merely existing in the source — is not decidable
/// here and is checked by `tool/run_standalone_examples.sh`, which reads the
/// group runners' output after running them.

/// Every self-announcement in the tree.
///
/// One per sample, normally: the banner `run()` opens with. A sample may print
/// more than one — a long one sometimes re-announces itself partway — and each
/// is checked, since each is a place to write the wrong number.

/// Samples that carry no banner, and so contribute an unlabelled block to
/// their group's transcript — or, if they print nothing at all, no block.
///
/// Twenty-seven of them printed nothing: they ran, asserted, exited 0, and left
/// no trace, so a reader watching the transcript could not have told they were
/// there. The reasonable conclusion from a runner's heading followed by silence
/// is that the samples were skipped, which is the same wrong answer the stale
/// ordinals gave, arrived at from the other direction.

/// Group runners printing a `NN: Title` label of their own.
///
/// A runner names the group; the samples name themselves. A runner that also
/// names each file writes a second title for it, in a second place, and the two
/// drift — ten runners carried such a label until five of them disagreed with
/// the sample they introduced, so one run of the suite announced a file under
/// two different ordinals.
///
/// Scanned as bare lines rather than paired with the call that follows: the
/// pairing was only needed to decide *which* sample a label belonged to, and
/// there is no longer a sample it may belong to.

variables.documentation:
/// A commented-out line, which does not call anything.
///
/// Commenting a call out is the quietest way to drop a sample from the suite —
/// nothing fails, the transcript is simply shorter — so a scan that counted
/// those as calls would miss the case it most needs to catch.

/// `import '03_shutdown_execution_example.dart' as shutdown_execution;`
///
/// Matched against the whole source rather than line by line, because the
