/// Builds the typed `List` of this shape from already-narrowed values.
///
/// The declared element type is what makes the decoded collection usable —
/// `List<Object>` would satisfy the cast in name only and fail the caller's
/// `as List<DateTime>` at the first use — and it is a thing only this row
/// knows, so it belongs here rather than in a switch the reader has to keep
/// in step with the table.

/// Builds the typed `Map` of this shape from already-narrowed values.
///
/// The key is always `String`; see [TomUserPreferenceCodec.stringType].

libraries.documentation:
/// Tom Execution Context - Zone-based context propagation system.
///
/// This module provides a mechanism for propagating contextual information
/// through asynchronous call chains using Dart's Zone system. It enables
/// access to user principal data, tenant information, and other request-scoped
/// values without explicit parameter passing.
///
/// ## Key Features
///
/// - **Zone-Based Propagation**: Context values flow through async boundaries
/// - **Context Composition**: Contexts can be combined and extended
/// - **Type-Safe Entries**: Context entries are typed and validated
/// - **Default Providers**: Built-in providers for common context values
///
/// ## Usage
///
/// ```dart
/// // Run code with context
/// final context = TomExecutionContext({
///   'userId': TomContextEntry<String>(() => '12345'),
/// });
///
/// context.runInContext(() {
///   // Access context values from anywhere in this zone, by entry name
///   final userId = TomExecutionContext.getValueFromCurrentZone<String>('userId');
/// });
/// ```
///
/// ## Two reads, and which one to use
///
/// A zone is keyed by `Type`, and [runInContext] installs exactly two keys:
/// [TomExecutionContext] and [TomZoneValues]. Everything else lives *inside*
/// one of those two containers, so which read applies depends on what is being
/// fetched:
///
/// - **The containers themselves** are read by type, with
///   `getFromCurrentZone<TomExecutionContext>()` and
///   `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()`.
/// - **A named context entry** is read by name, with
///   [TomExecutionContext.getValueFromCurrentZone]. Its type parameter selects
///   the entry's *value* type; it is not a zone key.
/// - **An ad-hoc zone value** passed as `zoneValues:` is read through the
///   container: `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId']`.
///
/// Reaching for the wrong one returns `null` rather than failing, so it is
/// worth being deliberate: `getFromCurrentZone<String>()` never sees an entry
/// named `'userId'`, however that entry is typed, because nothing ever
/// installs `String` as a zone key.

classes.documentation:
/// A zone-based execution context for propagating request-scoped values.
///
/// [TomExecutionContext] uses Dart's Zone system to propagate contextual
/// information through synchronous and asynchronous call chains without
/// explicit parameter passing.
///
/// ## How It Works
///
/// When code runs inside a context via [runInContext] or [runInContextAsync],
/// this context is installed in the zone under the [TomExecutionContext] type
/// key, so its entries are available anywhere in that zone. A single entry is
/// read by *name* with [getValueFromCurrentZone]; the context object itself is
/// read by *type* with `getFromCurrentZone<TomExecutionContext>()`. See the
/// library documentation for the full distinction.
///
/// ## Context Composition
///
/// Contexts can be nested and combined:
///
/// ```dart
/// final baseContext =
///     TomExecutionContext({'tenant': TomContextEntry<String>(() => 'acme')});
/// final userContext =
///     TomExecutionContext({'user': TomContextEntry<String>(() => 'john')});
///
/// baseContext.runInContext(() {
///   userContext.runInContext(() {
///     // Both 'tenant' and 'user' are available here
///   });
/// });
/// ```

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates an execution context with the given entries.
///
/// [contextMap] A map of entry names to [TomContextEntry] objects. Write each
/// entry's type parameter explicitly — `TomContextEntry<String>(...)`, not
/// `TomContextEntry(...)` — or it infers `dynamic` from this parameter's type
/// and no typed read will find it; see [TomContextEntry].

methods.documentation:
/// Gets a context entry by name.
/// [name] The name of the context entry.
/// Returns the [TomContextEntry] if found, or null.

/// Reads the entry named [name] from the [TomExecutionContext] installed in
/// the current zone, and returns its value.
///
/// This is the read for a context entry. [T] is the entry's *value* type, not
/// a zone key: the lookup finds the context by type and then the entry by
/// name, so `getFromCurrentZone<T>()` does not reach an entry (see the
/// library documentation).
///
/// Returns `null` when no context is installed, when no entry carries [name],
/// or when the entry found is not typed [T] — the same answer for all three,
/// so a caller that needs to tell them apart should use [getEntry] on the
/// context directly.

/// Adds or replaces a context entry.
///
/// [name] The unique name for this entry.
/// [entry] The context entry containing the value provider.

/// Merges another context's entries into this context.
///
/// Entries from [other] will overwrite entries with the same name.

/// Runs synchronous code within this context.
///
/// Creates a new zone whose typed context entries are this context's entries
/// merged with any [TomExecutionContext] already installed in the current
/// zone. On a colliding key **this context's entry wins** (the inner/child
/// context overrides the inherited one); the parent's map is never mutated.
///
/// The optional [zoneValues] are arbitrary, untyped zone values threaded
/// through alongside the typed context: they are wrapped in a
/// [TomZoneValues] container that is merged with any [TomZoneValues] already
/// in the current zone (existing values are preserved unless overridden by a
/// matching key in [zoneValues]). They are read back through that container —
/// `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId']` — and *not* with
/// [getValueFromCurrentZone], which looks in the context map.
///
/// Despite sharing its name, its type and its position with `runZoned`'s own
/// `zoneValues:`, this parameter does not install its keys in the zone
/// directly: they go into the [TomZoneValues] container, which — alongside the
/// [TomExecutionContext] — is the only thing installed. `Zone.current[key]`
/// therefore stays null for every key passed here, and that holds for a `Type`
/// key just as much as for a `String` one: `zoneValues: {TomPrincipal: p}`
/// does *not* make `getFromCurrentZone<TomPrincipal>()` resolve. (That form is
/// correct when calling `runZoned` directly, which is what makes it an easy
/// mistake to carry over — and a silent one, since the failed read yields null
/// rather than an error.)
///
/// The indirection is deliberate. Routing the values through a container is
/// what makes them *mutable* — code inside the zone can write
/// `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId'] = id` — and what makes
/// them *mergeable* across nested runs, since an inner `runInContext` folds
/// its values into the values it inherits instead of shadowing them wholesale.
/// Raw zone values are immutable once installed and replace rather than merge,
/// so neither is achievable by passing the map straight to `runZoned`.
///
/// Use [zoneValues] for ad-hoc values that do not warrant a typed
/// [TomContextEntry]; use the context map for the structured, named entries.
///
/// Example:
/// ```dart
/// final result = context.runInContext(() {
///   final user = TomExecutionContext.getValueFromCurrentZone<String>('userId');
///   final requestId = getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId'];
///   return computeResult(user, requestId);
/// }, zoneValues: {'requestId': id});
/// ```

/// Builds the zone-values map installed by [runInContext] /
/// [runInContextAsync].
///
/// The typed [TomExecutionContext] is this context's entries merged over any
/// context already in the current zone (this context wins on collision, the
/// parent's map is never mutated); the [TomZoneValues] container wraps
/// [zoneValues] merged with any [TomZoneValues] already in the current zone.

/// Runs asynchronous code within this context.
///
/// Creates a new zone whose typed context entries are this context's entries
/// merged with any [TomExecutionContext] already installed in the current
/// zone. On a colliding key **this context's entry wins** (the inner/child
/// context overrides the inherited one); the parent's map is never mutated.
///
/// This differs from [runInContext] only in its signature, not in the zone it
/// installs: [body] is typed `Future<T> Function()` and its future is handed
/// back, so `T` names the *awaited* value. Zone propagation is not the
/// distinction — the merged context and zone values remain available across
/// `await` boundaries under [runInContext] just as much as here, and reach
/// callbacks scheduled from the body that fire after the call has returned.
/// What this signature buys is that a body which drops its future is a
/// compile error rather than a silent early return.
///
/// The optional [zoneValues] are arbitrary, untyped zone values threaded
/// through alongside the typed context: they are wrapped in a
/// [TomZoneValues] container that is merged with any [TomZoneValues] already
/// in the current zone (existing values are preserved unless overridden by a
/// matching key in [zoneValues]). They are read back through that container —
/// `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId']` — and *not* with
/// [getValueFromCurrentZone], which looks in the context map. The keys are
/// not installed in the zone under themselves either, so `Zone.current[key]`
/// stays null — including for a `Type` key, which means
/// `zoneValues: {TomPrincipal: p}` does not make
/// `getFromCurrentZone<TomPrincipal>()` resolve. See [runInContext] for the
/// synchronous variant and for why the values are routed through a container.
///
/// Example:
/// ```dart
/// final result = await context.runInContextAsync(() async {
///   // Entries and zone values stay available here and across every await
///   final user = TomExecutionContext.getValueFromCurrentZone<String>('userId');
///   final requestId = getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?['requestId'];
///   return await fetchData(user, requestId);
/// }, zoneValues: {'requestId': id});
/// ```

classes.documentation:
/// A mutable container for zone values.
///
/// Provides indexed access to zone values for easier manipulation.
///
/// This container — not the caller's keys — is what
/// [TomExecutionContext.runInContext] installs in the zone, under the
/// `TomZoneValues` type key. A value threaded through `zoneValues:` is
/// therefore read back through the container,
/// `getFromCurrentZone<TomZoneValues>()?[key]`; `Zone.current[key]` stays null
/// whatever the key's type.
///
/// The extra hop buys the two things raw zone values cannot offer: the entries
/// stay **mutable** after the zone is entered, and a nested `runInContext`
/// **merges** its values into the inherited ones (see the `existingValues`
/// constructor argument) instead of replacing them.

/// A typed context entry with a lazy value provider.
///
/// [TomContextEntry] wraps a function that provides a context value.
/// The value is computed lazily when accessed.
///
/// ## Type Parameter
///
/// The type parameter [T] specifies the type of value this entry provides, and
/// [TomExecutionContext.getEntry] matches it exactly — an entry is only found
/// by a read for the very same [T].
///
/// **Always write [T] explicitly when registering an entry.** The context
/// constructor takes a `Map<String, TomContextEntry>`, which supplies
/// `TomContextEntry<dynamic>` as the downward inference context, so a raw
/// `TomContextEntry(() => 'acme')` written inside that map literal is a
/// `TomContextEntry<dynamic>` regardless of the value it returns — and every
/// typed read of it then yields `null`. (The same expression assigned to a
/// local infers `String`, which is what makes the trap easy to miss.)
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Static value
/// final entry = TomContextEntry<String>(() => 'constant');
///
/// // Dynamic value (computed each access)
/// final entry = TomContextEntry<DateTime>(() => DateTime.now());
///
/// // Value from zone
/// final entry = TomContextEntry<User>(() => getFromCurrentZone<User>());
/// ```

/// Static providers for common context values from [TomPrincipal].
///
/// These methods extract values from the current zone's [TomPrincipal] with
/// sensible defaults when no principal is present.
///
/// ## Available Providers
///
/// - **Organization/Application/Process**: Tenant identification
/// - **Language/Country/Timezone**: Server locale settings
/// - **ClientLanguage/ClientCountry/ClientTimezone**: Client locale settings
///
/// ## Field readers, not a locale derivation
///
/// Each provider reads **one** field and answers a sentinel when it is absent.
/// The stored pair and the device pair are reported separately and on purpose:
/// a caller that needs a specific claim — an audit entry recording what the
/// device said, a diagnostic naming what the user chose — must be able to ask
/// for that one and not be handed a blend.
///
/// Choosing *between* them is a different question with a different answer, and
/// these providers deliberately do not answer it. To pick the locale to render
/// text in for a recipient, use `TomTextLocale.fromPrincipal` in
/// `tom_core_server`'s resources module, which applies the precedence rule
/// (stored preference over device) and answers `null` — not a guess — when the
/// principal names no locale at all.
///
/// Composing these providers into a locale instead does not merely duplicate
/// that rule, it gets a different one: `TomTextLocale(getLanguage(),
/// getCountry())` yields `en_XX` for a principal that names nothing, and `XX`
/// is a sentinel rather than a country (see [getCountry]). That combination is
/// refused by `TomTextLocale`'s country invariant, so the mistake fails rather
/// than silently rendering through a bundle nobody asked for.

methods.documentation:
/// Reads a single field from the current zone's [TomPrincipal], falling back
/// to [fallback] when no principal is installed (or the field is null).
///
/// Centralises the principal-from-zone read shared by every provider getter,
/// so a change to how the principal is resolved lands in one place.

/// Returns the stored language from the current principal, or "en".
///
/// The fallback is a value, not a derived answer: it says the principal named
/// no language, not that this user reads English. A caller deciding what
/// language to *write to somebody in* wants `TomTextLocale.fromPrincipal`,
/// which distinguishes the two by answering `null`.

/// Returns the stored country from the current principal, or
/// [TomClientContext.noCountry] — the non-locale sentinel described there,
/// which a consumer building a locale key must drop rather than treat as a
/// region.

/// Returns the client country from the current principal, or
/// [TomClientContext.noCountry].
///
/// Here the sentinel is often the *stored* value rather than the fallback:
/// [TomAuthenticationMessage.clientCountry] starts there, so a device that
/// names no region sends it and it reaches the principal through an ordinary
/// sign-in.

variables.documentation:
/// Pre-configured execution context with standard context providers.
///
/// This context provides access to common principal-based values:
/// - `organization`, `application`, `process`
/// - `language`, `country`, `timezone`
/// - `clientLanguage`, `clientCountry`, `clientTimezone`
///
/// ## Usage
///
/// ```dart
/// tomExecutionContext.runInContext(() {
///   // Access organization, language, etc. by entry name
///   final org = TomExecutionContext.getValueFromCurrentZone<String>('organization');
/// });
/// ```
///
/// `final`, and deliberately so. Nothing in the workspace replaces it, the
/// dartdoc above describes only reading it, and every downstream re-export
/// already exposes it read-only — `tom_build_cli`'s `D4rtGlobals` publishes a
/// getter, and the d4rt bridge registers the same. A reassignable global would
/// let one importer swap the ambient context out from under every other, which
/// is a coupling nobody asked for and nothing uses. Build a `TomExecutionContext`
/// of your own and call `runInContext` on it when you need different providers.

libraries.documentation:
/// Declarative jobs — a class annotated with [TomScheduledJob] *is* its own
/// registration.
///
/// Without this library a job is declared twice: once as the class that does
/// the work, and once as a hand-written [TomJobDefinition] at start-up that
/// wires a closure to a name, a schedule and a set of policies. Nothing relates
/// the two. A job class can exist and never be registered; a registration can
/// out-live the class it names; and a rename silently breaks the pairing,
/// because the only thing joining them is a string a human typed twice.
///
/// Here the class carries its own schedule:
///
/// ```dart
/// @tomReflector
/// @TomScheduledJob(cron: '0 3 * * *')
/// class NightlyReconciliation extends TomJobBase {
///   @override
///   Future<Object?> execute() async { /* ... */ }
/// }
/// ```
///
/// and start-up becomes one line:
///
/// ```dart
/// TomScheduledJobs.registerAll(scheduler);
/// ```
///
/// ## Why reflection is the right answer *here*
///
/// The kernel deliberately does **not** discover file-storage backends by
/// annotation (see `blob_store_registry.dart`): a backend is routinely the
/// deployment's own, its factory is a closure precisely so it can capture a
/// configured HTTP client or credential provider, and an annotation would put a
/// codegen step in front of a one-line registration.
///
/// None of those three apply to a job. A job is application code that is
/// already inside the application's own reflection build; its settings are
/// literals — a cron string, a retry count, a timeout — that an annotation
/// expresses natively; and it needs no captured collaborators, because the body
/// is built fresh per run and can resolve what it needs from the bean locator
/// (see [TomJobBase]). The closure in [TomJobDefinition.body] exists only to
/// make the body *per-run*, and `newInstance('', [])` per run does that.
///
/// ## Everything is checked at scan time
///
/// This is the whole robustness claim. Quartz stores a job's class as a
/// **string** (`QRTZ_JOB_DETAILS.JOB_CLASS_NAME`) and resolves it at fire time:
/// a rename parks the trigger in `ERROR` permanently, and cluster recovery
/// silently skips a firing whose class no longer resolves. A reflected class is
/// present at compile time — the class *is* the registration, so a rename is
/// just a rename — and every remaining way to get a declaration wrong is
/// reported by [TomScheduledJobs.discover] at start-up, naming the offending
/// class, rather than at 03:00 into a log nobody reads.
///
/// ## Discovery produces definitions; it does not replace them
///
/// [TomJobDefinition] and [TomScheduler] are untouched by this library. A job
/// whose schedule is computed from configuration, or whose body has to close
/// over something, can never be an annotation — so a hand-written definition
/// stays fully registrable and the two coexist in one scheduler.

classes.documentation:
/// Raised when an annotated job cannot be turned into a [TomJobDefinition].
///
/// Always thrown from the scan, never from a firing, and always naming the
/// class at fault — the point of the annotation route is that a malformed
/// declaration stops start-up instead of failing in the small hours.

/// The base class for a declaratively scheduled job.
///
/// A job is a [TomCommand] — that is what the scheduler dispatches, and nothing
/// about that changes here. [TomJobBase] adds the one thing a command built by
/// reflection cannot be handed through its constructor: the [run] it was
/// created for. Extending it is therefore optional in the strict sense (any
/// [TomCommand] with a no-argument constructor is accepted), but a job that
/// wants to know its window, its attempt number or its event payload has to.
///
/// ## Why the constructor takes no arguments
///
/// The body is instantiated per run by `newInstance('', [])`, so there is no
/// call site to pass collaborators from. Resolve them instead — in the
/// constructor or in [execute] — from the bean locator:
///
/// ```dart
/// @tomReflector
/// @TomScheduledJob(cron: '@daily')
/// class ExpireSessions extends TomJobBase {
///   final TomBean<SessionService> _sessions = TomBean<SessionService>();
///
///   @override
///   Future<Object?> execute() async =>
///       _sessions.get().expireBefore(run.scheduledFor);
/// }
/// ```
///
/// That is not a workaround for the annotation; it is the same wiring every
/// other framework component uses, and it keeps the job's dependencies
/// resolvable in a worker isolate, where a captured reference would not have
/// survived the copy.

fields.documentation:
/// The run this instance was created for.
///
/// Assigned by [TomScheduledJobs] immediately after construction and before
/// [execute] is called. Reading it from an instance built by hand — in a unit
/// test, say — throws, which is the honest answer: there is no run.

classes.documentation:
/// Declares that the annotated class is a scheduled job.
///
/// Carries what [TomJobDefinition] takes as constructor arguments, with one
/// unavoidable difference: a [TomSchedule] is not a constant, so it cannot be
/// an annotation argument. The trigger is instead declared by four **mutually
/// exclusive** fields — [cron], [interval], [calendar] and [event] — of which
/// exactly one must be set. Setting none, or two, is a scan-time error.
///
/// The class must also carry `@tomReflector`: the reflection substrate exposes
/// only `annotatedClasses`, with no by-annotation and no by-supertype query, so
/// a job that is not in the reflection build is not discoverable at all.

fields.documentation:
/// The job's identity, defaulting to the class's simple name.
///
/// **This is the store key.** Runs in a [TomJobStore] are keyed by it, so
/// renaming the class of a job that already has stored runs orphans them —
/// the history stays under the old name and the job re-anchors as if new.
/// Pin the old name here when renaming a job that is already in production;
/// that is the entire reason this field is overridable.

/// A fixed period, e.g. `const Duration(minutes: 15)`.
///
/// Fire times are computed from the Unix epoch by whole multiples of the
/// period, so they are reproducible across processes and do not drift after
/// an outage.

/// A calendar expression — the month-relative rules cron cannot spell.
///
/// Whitespace-separated `key:value` terms, exactly one of which selects the
/// rule:
///
/// | Term | Meaning |
/// | ---- | ------- |
/// | `day:<n>` | day of month, `1`..`31`, or `-1`..`-31` counting from the month's end |
/// | `weekday:<ordinal>/<day>` | ordinal `1`..`5` or `last`; day `mon`..`sun` |
/// | `at:<HH:MM>` | time of day, default `00:00` |
/// | `every:<months>` | month interval `1`..`12`, default `1` |
/// | `from:<month>` | the month `1`..`12` the interval is anchored on, default `1` |
///
/// For example `'day:-1 at:23:30'` is the last day of every month at 23:30,
/// and `'weekday:2/tue at:09:00 every:3'` is the second Tuesday of every
/// third month at 09:00.

/// Declares the job event-triggered rather than clock-driven.
///
/// A stream is not a constant either, so the source is supplied at scan time
/// through [TomScheduledJobs.discover]'s `eventSource` resolver, keyed by job
/// name. An event job discovered without a resolvable source is a scan-time
/// error, not a job that quietly never fires.

/// Whether a run interrupted mid-execution may be re-queued.
///
/// `false` by default — see [TomJobDefinition.requestsRecovery]. Set it only
/// on a body that is safe to run twice; a run that was claimed but never
/// started is re-queued either way.

classes.documentation:
/// Turns [TomScheduledJob]-annotated classes into registered jobs.
///
/// See the library documentation for why discovery is the recommended way to
/// declare a job and why it leaves [TomJobDefinition] and [TomScheduler]
/// untouched.

methods.documentation:
/// Scans the reflected classes and returns one definition per annotated job.
///
/// Validates every declaration before returning any of them, so a start-up
/// that has one bad job registers none of them rather than half of them.
///
/// [eventSource] supplies the stream for a job declared `event: true`, keyed
/// by the job's resolved name; returning `null` (or omitting the resolver)
/// makes such a job a scan-time error.
///
/// [where] narrows the scan to the classes it accepts. The default — every
/// reflected class — is what an application wants; a deployment that ships
/// one binary but runs different jobs per profile filters here rather than
/// registering and then deregistering, and the validation suite uses it to
/// examine one malformed declaration at a time.

/// Discovers every annotated job and registers it on [scheduler].
///
/// Refuses to overwrite a name [scheduler] already knows: a discovered job
/// silently replacing a hand-written one — or the reverse, depending on
/// start-up order — is exactly the kind of pairing bug the annotation exists
/// to remove. Registration happens only after the whole scan has validated.
///
/// Returns the definitions it registered.

/// How a class is identified in a scan failure.
///
/// The qualified name where there is one — two classes with the same simple
/// name in different libraries are exactly the case worth disambiguating —
/// but a mirror whose library is unnamed reports a bare `.ClassName`, and
/// leading punctuation in an error message reads like a typo.

/// Parses a [TomScheduledJob.calendar] expression.
///
/// Strict on purpose: an unknown term, a duplicated term or a malformed value
/// is rejected rather than ignored, because a silently dropped `at:` term
/// would move a month-end job to midnight and nothing would say so.

libraries.documentation:
/// Schedule expressions — the *when* half of a background job.
///
/// A CE-JB job declares a trigger of one of three kinds (`cron | calendar |
/// event`). This library turns each of those into something the scheduler can
/// ask a single question of: **given that I last considered the world at time
/// T, when does this job next want to run?**
///
/// That question — [TomSchedule.nextFireAfter] — is deliberately the whole
/// contract. A schedule computes fire *times*; it never holds a timer, never
/// touches a clock of its own, and never knows whether a computed window
/// actually ran. All of that belongs to the scheduler, which means a schedule
/// is a pure function and can be tested by evaluating it against fixed dates
/// with no waiting and no fakes.
///
/// ## The shipped schedules
///
/// * [TomCronSchedule] — the standard five-field POSIX cron expression, for
///   "at these minutes of these hours of these days".
/// * [TomCalendarSchedule] — recurrences cron *cannot* express, which is
///   exactly why it is a separate kind rather than a second spelling of the
///   first: last-day-of-month, and the nth (or last) weekday of a month.
/// * [TomIntervalSchedule] — a fixed period from an anchor, for cadences that
///   are not aligned to the wall clock at all ("every 90 seconds").
/// * [TomOnceSchedule] — a single absolute instant, then never again. The only
///   non-recurring clock-driven schedule, and the one that expresses a deadline
///   or a timer wait.
/// * [TomEventSchedule] — not clock-driven. It answers `null` to every
///   [TomSchedule.nextFireAfter] and instead exposes a [Stream]; the scheduler
///   subscribes and enqueues a run per event.
///
/// ## Time zones
///
/// Every schedule answers in the zone it was asked in: a UTC [DateTime] in
/// gives a UTC one out, a local one gives local. That is the *flag* only, and
/// the two recurring families differ in what the zone goes on to mean.
///
/// * [TomCronSchedule] and [TomCalendarSchedule] read and write **wall-clock
///   fields**, so a local `0 3 * * *` means 03:00 civil time. Across a DST
///   boundary they hold 03:00, and the gap to the previous run becomes 23 or
///   25 real hours.
/// * [TomIntervalSchedule] computes in **epoch microseconds** and never
///   consults the zone, so a 24-hour period is always 24 real hours and its
///   wall-clock time shifts by an hour across that same boundary.
///
/// Asked in UTC, both give the same DST-immune absolute cadence, because there
/// is no civil time left to disagree about. The schedule does not decide which
/// you want — the scheduler's clock does.
///
/// That mirror image is this module's most surprising property, and no unit
/// test pins it. Each half follows from its own arithmetic rather than from a
/// policy that could drift: the cron half from the local branch of `_at`, the
/// interval half from epoch arithmetic that cannot see a zone at all. A DST
/// assertion would therefore be re-testing `DateTime`, and pinning one for
/// real needs a zone-controlled process, which the fleet's Windows host cannot
/// run. What the suite does pin is the mechanism on each side — that cron
/// reads its fields in the caller's zone, and that the interval grid is
/// absolute — which is what the DST behaviour is a consequence of.
///
/// [TomOnceSchedule] is the exception that proves the rule: it names one
/// absolute instant rather than a wall-clock position, so there is nothing for
/// a zone to change. It still answers in [from]'s zone, but only because the
/// contract says the answer is comparable to the question.

classes.documentation:
/// A job trigger, reduced to the one question the scheduler asks of it.
///
/// Implementations must be **pure**: [nextFireAfter] may not read a clock, and
/// two calls with the same argument must return the same answer. The scheduler
/// relies on that to replay windows deterministically after a restart.

methods.documentation:
/// The first fire time **strictly after** [from], or `null` when this
/// schedule will never fire again.
///
/// `null` is a real answer, not an error: `0 0 30 2 *` (February 30th) can
/// never match, and an [TomEventSchedule] is never clock-due. Callers must
/// treat it as "stop asking", not as a failure.
///
/// The returned value is in the same zone as [from] — UTC in, UTC out.

classes.documentation:
/// A standard five-field POSIX cron expression.
///
/// ```text
/// ┌───────── minute        (0-59)
/// │ ┌─────── hour          (0-23)
/// │ │ ┌───── day of month  (1-31)
/// │ │ │ ┌─── month         (1-12 or JAN-DEC)
/// │ │ │ │ ┌─ day of week   (0-7 or SUN-SAT, both 0 and 7 mean Sunday)
/// * * * * *
/// ```
///
/// Each field accepts `*`, a single value, a `low-high` range, a comma-separated
/// list of either, and a `/step` suffix on `*` or on a range (`*/15`, `1-10/2`).
/// Month and day-of-week names are accepted case-insensitively.
///
/// ## Two behaviours worth knowing before you write an expression
///
/// **Day-of-month and day-of-week are OR-ed, not AND-ed.** This is POSIX
/// behaviour and it surprises people every time: when *both* fields are
/// restricted, the expression fires on a day matching *either*. `0 0 1 * MON`
/// is "midnight on the 1st, **and** every Monday" — not "the 1st when it is a
/// Monday". When only one of the two is restricted, only that one applies.
/// [TomCalendarSchedule] exists partly so that intersecting rules have a
/// spelling that means what it looks like.
///
/// A **step restricts**, which decides where that rule lands: `0 0 */2 * *`
/// fires on odd days, and `0 0 */2 * MON` is odd days *or* Mondays. Only a
/// bare `*` — or `*/1`, the same thing spelled with a no-op step — leaves a
/// day field unrestricted. Vixie cron differs here, reading every `*/n` as
/// unrestricted and so ANDing that example into odd Mondays.
///
/// **There is no seconds field.** Not on either end — a six-field expression is
/// rejected rather than guessed at, because the two common conventions put
/// seconds at opposite ends and a misread expression fires 60× too often. A
/// sub-minute cadence is [TomIntervalSchedule]'s job.
///
/// ## Macros
///
/// `@yearly`/`@annually`, `@monthly`, `@weekly`, `@daily`/`@midnight` and
/// `@hourly` expand to their conventional expressions.

methods.documentation:
/// Whether [field] as written restricts nothing, which is what decides the
/// OR question in [_matchesDay].
///
/// A step expression restricts. POSIX words the day rule as applying when
/// both day fields are "not `*`", and `*/2` is not `*` — it names half the
/// days. Reading it as unrestricted computes that restriction and then
/// throws it away, which makes `0 0 */2 * *` fire *every* day.
///
/// `*/1` is the one step that does not restrict: it is a wildcard with a
/// redundant step, expands to every value, and counting it as a restriction
/// would pull the other day field into an OR its author plainly did not ask
/// for. The line is therefore drawn at how the field is *written* — a plain
/// wildcard, with or without the no-op step — and not at which values it
/// happens to cover, so an explicit `1-31` restricts like any other range.
///
/// This is stricter than Vixie cron, which tests only a field's first
/// character and so counts every `*/n` as unrestricted. Under that reading
/// `0 0 */2 * MON` means odd days *and* Mondays; under the POSIX wording,
/// which is the rule this class documents, it means odd days *or* Mondays.

/// Applies the POSIX day rule: OR when both day fields are restricted,
/// otherwise whichever one is restricted. See [_isUnrestricted] for where
/// that line falls.

fields.documentation:
/// How far ahead [nextFireAfter] searches before concluding "never".
///
/// Nine years, which is what lets `null` be read as *never* rather than *not
/// within the window*. The rarest date a cron expression can name is February
/// 29th, and the longest gap between two of them is **eight** years, not
/// four: 2100 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so the leap day skips from
/// 2096 to 2104. The limit is anchored on the search start's own month and
/// day and the scan is half-open, so a search beginning *on* a February 29th
/// needs one year more than the gap it crosses — hence nine.
///
/// Every other combination of fields recurs at least once a year, so nine
/// covers the whole expression language and a `null` means the expression is
/// unsatisfiable (`0 0 30 2 *`) rather than merely distant.

classes.documentation:
/// Recurrences that cron cannot express.
///
/// This type exists for a narrow reason: cron's day-of-month field counts
/// forward from the 1st and has no way to say "the last day of the month" (the
/// 31st is not the last day of April) or "the second Tuesday". Those two rules
/// cover the great majority of real calendar-driven work — month-end billing,
/// period close, a report on the first Monday — and neither can be written as a
/// cron expression at all.
///
/// Anything cron *can* say should be said with [TomCronSchedule]; having two
/// spellings for one recurrence is how two jobs end up disagreeing about what
/// "daily" means.

constructors.documentation:
/// Fires on [dayOfMonth] of every [monthInterval]-th month at [hour]:[minute].
///
/// Pass a negative [dayOfMonth] to count from the end of the month: `-1` is
/// the last day, `-2` the second to last. This is the spelling cron lacks.

/// Fires on the [ordinal]-th [weekday] of every [monthInterval]-th month.
///
/// [weekday] uses Dart's constants (`DateTime.monday`..`DateTime.sunday`).
/// [ordinal] is `1`-`5`, or `-1` for the last occurrence in the month. A
/// 5th occurrence that a given month does not have simply does not fire.

methods.documentation:
/// The day-of-month of the [ordinal]-th [weekday] in the given month, or
/// `-1` when the month has no such occurrence.
///
/// Only reached under [TomCalendarRule.weekdayOfMonth], which is what makes
/// both fields non-null here.

fields.documentation:
/// How far ahead [nextFireAfter] searches before concluding "never".
///
/// Nine years, in months, for the reason given on [TomCronSchedule]'s
/// horizon: `dayOfMonth(29, monthInterval: 12, anchorMonth: 2)` names
/// February 29th, so this class has to clear the same eight-year gap across a
/// century that is not a leap year.

/// Which rule this schedule applies, and therefore which fields are set.
///
/// One class serves both rules, so the fields the chosen rule does not use
/// are `null` rather than filled with a stand-in. A placeholder [ordinal] of
/// `1` would read as a genuine first-occurrence schedule and give a caller no
/// way to notice it was never asked for; `null` cannot be mistaken for an
/// answer, which is what stops a reader disagreeing with this field.

/// The day of the month, or `null` under [TomCalendarRule.weekdayOfMonth].
///
/// Counted from the start of the month when positive and from its end when
/// negative (`-1` = last day).
///
/// A positive day that does not exist in a given month (the 31st of April)
/// simply does not fire that month — it is **not** clamped, because a
/// month-end job that silently ran on the 30th would be indistinguishable
/// from one that ran on time.

classes.documentation:
/// A fixed period measured from an anchor instant.
///
/// Use this when the cadence is not aligned to the wall clock — "every 90
/// seconds", "every 45 minutes" — which cron cannot express because its fields
/// are calendar positions, not durations.
///
/// Fire times are computed from [anchor] by whole multiples of [period], not by
/// repeatedly adding to the previous answer, so a scheduler that was down for
/// an hour resumes on the original grid instead of drifting by the outage.

/// A single absolute instant: fire once at [at], then never again.
///
/// The other clock-driven schedules all recur. This one is what expresses a
/// **deadline** — "escalate this ticket at 09:00 on the 1st", "expire this
/// invitation in seven days" — and it is the shape a timer wait takes when a
/// multi-step process is built out of jobs rather than out of a process engine.
/// Without it, a caller needing one future instant has to abuse
/// [TomIntervalSchedule] (which will fire again) or write a subclass at the
/// call site.
///
/// It needs no scheduler support because [TomSchedule] already accommodates it:
/// `null` from [nextFireAfter] means *never again*, so the whole implementation
/// is "the instant, if we are not past it yet".
///
/// ## An instant, not a wall-clock position
///
/// [at] is an absolute moment. Its zone affects nothing — a deadline of
/// `2026-08-01T09:00Z` is the same moment whether it is compared against a UTC
/// or a local clock, and unlike the recurring schedules there is no DST
/// question to answer because there is no recurrence to shift. [nextFireAfter]
/// still returns its answer in [from]'s zone, so the value can be compared and
/// stored like any other schedule's, but that is a rendering, not a
/// reinterpretation. [describe] prints UTC for the same reason: a deadline in an
/// alert should not depend on which host formatted it.
///
/// ## Nothing to validate, including a deadline in the past
///
/// The sibling schedules reject expressions that cannot mean anything. A
/// [DateTime] always means something, *including one already behind us*: that
/// is a schedule which will never fire, and `null` is exactly how the contract
/// spells it. The schedule cannot judge otherwise without reading a clock, and
/// [nextFireAfter] must stay pure — the scheduler replays windows
/// deterministically after a restart and would get different answers from a
/// schedule that had opinions about now.
///
/// For the same reason there is deliberately no `TomOnceSchedule.after(
/// Duration)` convenience. It would have to read the clock *at construction*,
/// and job definitions are typically rebuilt at start-up — so a "fire in three
/// days" schedule would quietly push its own deadline three days further out on
/// every restart, and a process that restarted often would never fire at all.
/// Compute the instant once, persist it, and pass it here.
///
/// ## Being planned is what survives a restart
///
/// A one-shot fires late rather than not at all when the process is down over
/// its instant, because the scheduler enqueues the window as soon as it sees
/// the job — the run is then durable store state, and
/// [TomMissedWindowPolicy.skip] does not un-plan it. The case that genuinely
/// does not fire is a job **first registered after** its instant has passed:
/// first sight anchors on the present for every schedule kind, so there is no
/// window to catch up on. The scheduler raises
/// [TomJobAlertKind.neverFires] for that rather than staying quiet about it.

/// A trigger fed by a source rather than a clock.
///
/// CE-JB's third trigger kind. [nextFireAfter] always answers `null` and
/// [isClockDriven] is `false`, so the scheduler never polls it; instead it
/// subscribes to [events] and enqueues one run per emitted element.
///
/// The emitted value is available to the job body as the run's payload, so an
/// event-triggered job can act on *what* happened rather than merely *that*
/// something did.

functions.documentation:
/// Builds a [DateTime] in the same zone as [reference].
///
/// Out-of-range components are normalised by the `DateTime` constructor (month
/// 13 becomes January of the next year, day 32 rolls into the next month),
/// which is what lets the search loops step forward by simply incrementing a
/// component instead of doing calendar arithmetic by hand.

libraries.documentation:
/// The scheduler runtime — the loop that turns schedules into dispatched work.
///
/// Everything the scheduler does happens in one method, [TomScheduler.tick].
/// [TomScheduler.start] merely arranges for it to be called periodically. That
/// shape is deliberate: a runtime whose behaviour is only reachable through a
/// timer can only be tested by waiting, and a test that waits for a nightly job
/// is a test nobody writes. Here the entire loop — window planning, catch-up,
/// claiming, dispatch, timeout, retry and alerting — is a single awaitable call
/// driven by an injectable clock, so a year of scheduling is exercised in
/// milliseconds and the code under test is the code that runs in production.
///
/// ## One pass, in order
///
/// 1. **Recover.** Runs whose lease expired go back to `pending`. This is how a
///    run that was in flight when the process died becomes visible again.
/// 2. **Plan.** For each clock-driven job, work out which windows have elapsed
///    since the last one on record and enqueue them per the job's
///    [TomMissedWindowPolicy], then enqueue the next future window. Enqueueing
///    is keyed by window, so this is idempotent and a replayed tick is a no-op.
/// 3. **Dispatch.** Claim the due runs, hand each body to the
///    [TomJobDispatcher], and record the outcome — success, a retry with
///    backoff, or terminal failure with an alert.
///
/// ## What this runtime does not do
///
/// It does not execute anything. Bodies go to a dispatcher, which in production
/// is the kernel's existing isolate pool.
///
/// ## Running more than one of these
///
/// Two schedulers pointed at the same store will both claim the same due run
/// unless something arbitrates between them. Supply a [TomLeaseLock] and this
/// scheduler becomes cluster-safe: every run is gated on a lease, the lease is
/// renewed while the work runs, and a node that dies has its work picked up when
/// its lease lapses. Leave the lock `null` — the default — and the scheduler
/// behaves exactly as a single-node runtime, with [TomJobDefinition.allowOverlap]
/// serialising a job's runs within this process only.
///
/// The execution guarantee is **at-least-once**, and it is stated in full in
/// `doc/scheduling/scheduling.md`. Job bodies must be idempotent. The lock makes
/// duplicate execution rare and bounded; it does not make it impossible, and a
/// runtime that claimed otherwise would be lying about a case its users will
/// eventually hit.

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a scheduler.
///
/// [ownerId] defaults to a value derived from the process start time, which
/// is enough to tell one scheduler's leases from another's in the store.
/// **In a cluster it must be distinct per node**, and the default satisfies
/// that only by luck — two nodes started in the same microsecond would
/// collide. Set it explicitly to something stable and unique, such as the
/// host name plus the process id.
///
/// [leaseRenewInterval] defaults to a third of [leaseTtl], which leaves room
/// for two renewals to fail before the lease lapses.

methods.documentation:
/// Registers [job], replacing any previous definition of the same name.
///
/// An [TomEventSchedule] job is subscribed immediately, so events that arrive
/// before [start] are still enqueued — they are just not dispatched until a
/// tick runs.

/// Removes [name] from the registry and cancels any event subscription.
///
/// Runs already in the store are left alone: they were planned, and dropping
/// them silently would make deregistration a way to lose work.

/// Cancels [subscription] without waiting for it.
///
/// Cancellation of a broadcast/controller subscription completes on a later
/// microtask; nothing downstream depends on that completion, and making
/// [register], [deregister] and [close] async just to await it would put an
/// await point in the middle of registry mutation.

/// Runs one [tick] immediately, then repeats roughly every [tickInterval].
///
/// The immediate pass matters on restart: it is what recovers abandoned
/// leases and catches up missed windows without waiting out a full interval.
///
/// "Roughly" is [tickJitter]: each interval is shortened by an independent
/// random fraction, so a cluster started together does not stay in lockstep.

/// Arms a one-shot timer for the next pass and re-arms from its own callback.
///
/// A one-shot chain rather than [Timer.periodic] because each interval is
/// drawn separately — a periodic timer can only have one period, and a jitter
/// applied once is a constant offset, not jitter.

/// Stops the periodic timer. In-flight runs are left to finish.
///
/// Returns immediately: it stops new passes, not the work already started.
/// `stop()` followed by `await drain()` is the pair that quiesces a
/// scheduler, and in that order — draining one that is still ticking races
/// the timer for what "idle" means.

/// Stops the scheduler, cancels event subscriptions and closes the
/// dispatcher.
///
/// Runs already dispatched are not waited for — [close] is the shutdown path,
/// and their leases will expire so another process picks them up. A shutdown
/// that would rather finish the work than hand it on calls [stop] and awaits
/// [drain] first; [close] itself cannot, being synchronous.
///
/// Held leases are released on the way out so peers can take the work
/// immediately instead of waiting out [leaseTtl]. That release is
/// fire-and-forget: [TomClosable.close] is synchronous, and a lease that fails
/// to release simply expires, which is the behaviour a hard kill would have
/// produced anyway.

/// Runs one complete scheduling pass, **starting** the runs it finds due.
///
/// Safe to call directly — this is what unit tests drive. Overlapping calls
/// are collapsed: a pass still running when the timer fires again is not
/// re-entered, because two concurrent planners would both see the same window
/// as unplanned.
///
/// Awaiting this does **not** await the work. A pass recovers, plans, claims
/// and dispatches; the bodies it dispatched are still running when it
/// returns, deliberately, so that a slow job cannot hold up the next window
/// of a fast one. To wait for them, follow it with [drain].

/// Completes once every run this scheduler has in flight has finished.
///
/// The other half of [tick]. A pass returns as soon as the work is started,
/// which is right for a timer and useless to anyone who needs to know the
/// work is *done* — a test asserting an effect, a shutdown path that wants
/// the queue emptied before it closes, or a caller like
/// `TomMessageOutbox.tick()` whose own contract is "the pass is complete".
/// Without this they had only `pumpEventQueue()` and a guess.
///
/// "Finished" means the attempt returned and its outcome was written to the
/// store — success, a scheduled retry, or terminal failure — not that the run
/// succeeded. A drain reports that nothing is still executing here; the store
/// reports what happened.
///
/// Re-checks after each wave, because a run that finishes may leave another
/// claimable and a concurrent pass may have started more. It therefore
/// completes only when the scheduler is genuinely idle, which also means a
/// caller draining a *running* scheduler is racing the timer: call [stop]
/// first if the intent is to quiesce.

/// Returns expired leases to the queue and alerts on each.
///
/// The scheduler is the one caller that can see the job registry, so it is the
/// one caller that can answer [TomJobDefinition.requestsRecovery] — a store
/// asked in isolation would have to guess. A job the registry does not know
/// answers `false`, which is the safe reading: an unregistered job is one this
/// node cannot run anyway, and declining is reversible where a repeated side
/// effect is not.

/// Enqueues whatever [job] owes: missed windows per its policy, plus the next
/// future window.
///
/// The two halves are anchored differently on purpose. Catch-up is anchored
/// on the last window *on record*, because that is what defines "missed".
/// Look-ahead is anchored on **now**, not on the record — anchoring it on the
/// record too would enqueue one window further into the future on every tick,
/// walking the plan forward forever.

/// Enqueues the missed windows [job]'s policy asks for, dating each run to
/// the window it belongs to but making it due immediately.
///
/// Returns whether the whole backlog was enqueued. `false` means the pass's
/// shared [maxCatchUpPerTick] budget ran out and the remainder is left for the
/// next pass — see the caller for why that requires skipping look-ahead.

/// Adds a run for [scheduledFor] unless one already exists.
///
/// The existence check is the idempotence guarantee that makes a tick safe to
/// replay after a crash.
///
/// Returns whether a run was actually added, which is what lets the catch-up
/// budget count work rather than attempts.

/// Enqueues a run for an event-triggered job, carrying [payload].
///
/// Each event gets its own window keyed by arrival time, so two events in the
/// same instant collapse into one run and a rapid source cannot flood the
/// queue with duplicates of the same moment.

/// The resource a run's coordination lease is taken on.
///
/// The key *is* the policy. A job that forbids overlap leases its own name,
/// so the whole cluster runs at most one of it at a time — the cluster-wide
/// reading of [TomJobDefinition.allowOverlap]. A job that allows overlap
/// leases the individual run, so concurrent windows are fine but no single
/// window is executed twice.

/// Wins the coordination lease for [run], or reports why it could not.
///
/// Returns `null` both when there is no lock (the single-node case, where the
/// caller proceeds anyway) and when a peer holds the lease; the caller
/// distinguishes them by whether [lock] is set.

/// Executes one attempt of the run held by [claim] and records the outcome.
///
/// The *claim* is threaded through rather than the run, because every write
/// below is fenced on it: this node may have been partitioned long enough for
/// the lease to lapse, and the outcome it is about to record then belongs to
/// a peer's attempt rather than to its own. See the rule on [TomJobStore].
///
/// [lease] is the coordination lease held for this run, or `null` when the
/// scheduler is running single-node.

/// Whether this node may still write [run]'s outcome to the store.
///
/// Once a lease is lost the run belongs to whichever node took it over.
/// Writing our result anyway would clobber theirs — marking succeeded a run
/// they are still executing, or failing one they already completed. Our work
/// is not wasted, it is simply no longer authoritative, and the discrepancy
/// is reported as [TomJobAlertKind.leaseLost] rather than hidden.

/// Starts the renewal timer that keeps [lease] alive while the work runs.
///
/// Renewal is what decouples the lease TTL from job duration: without it the
/// TTL would have to exceed the slowest possible run, and a dead node would
/// then block its work for exactly that long.

/// Fails one attempt, scheduling a retry when the policy allows one.
///
/// Two things can cut the retry budget short: exhausting it, and an error the
/// body classified as [TomPermanentFailure]. The second is checked first
/// because it is the stronger statement — "no attempt will ever succeed"
/// makes "you have two attempts left" irrelevant.

fields.documentation:
/// How many windows the catch-up scan will step through before giving up.
///
/// A guard, not a policy: without it, a job with a minutely schedule whose
/// store was last written a year ago would spin through half a million
/// iterations on its first tick. At the default this is roughly ten weeks of
/// minutely windows, far beyond any outage worth catching up on.

/// How long a claim may go unrefreshed before the run is considered
/// abandoned and returned to the queue.
///
/// Must comfortably exceed the longest [TomJobDefinition.timeout] in the
/// registry, or a slow-but-healthy run will be handed to a second attempt
/// while the first is still going. Positive — only the lower bound can be
/// checked here, since the registry is not populated until [register].

/// The most runs claimed in one pass.
///
/// At least 1. Zero is not "no limit": the claim stops once it holds this
/// many, so a zero budget claims nothing on every pass while planning keeps
/// filling the queue behind it.

/// The most catch-up runs enqueued in one pass, across **all** jobs.
///
/// [TomJobDefinition.catchUpLimit] bounds one job's backlog; this bounds the
/// whole registry's, and the difference matters after a long outage: fifty
/// jobs each within their own limit still add up to a planning pass that runs
/// for minutes and a queue in which nothing due *now* can be seen for the
/// backlog. Quartz caps the same thing with `maxMisfiresToHandleAtATime`, and
/// this default is its default.
///
/// Clipping **defers**, it does not drop. A job whose catch-up was clipped
/// skips its look-ahead enqueue for that pass, which leaves the last window on
/// record behind the backlog so the next pass resumes exactly where this one
/// stopped. The work arrives a tick or two later; none of it is lost.

/// How much of [tickInterval] may be shaved off at random, `0`-`1`.
///
/// Jitter — not partitioning — is the answer to N nodes waking together. A
/// cluster started by the same deployment ticks in lockstep otherwise, and
/// every node then contends for the same claims in the same millisecond; the
/// contention is harmless but the wasted round-trips are not free, and they
/// grow with the node count.
///
/// Applied **downward only**, so a tick never falls later than [tickInterval]
/// and the interval keeps its meaning as a maximum. Set it to `0` for an exact
/// period.

/// Cluster coordination, or `null` for a single-node scheduler.
///
/// When present, no run is executed without first winning its lease, so two
/// schedulers sharing a store cannot both execute the same run.

/// How long a coordination lease is taken for.
///
/// Short is good: it bounds how long a dead node blocks its work. It is safe
/// to keep it short because [leaseRenewInterval] pushes it forward for as long
/// as the work is genuinely running, so this does **not** need to exceed the
/// slowest job. Positive, and strictly above [leaseRenewInterval].

/// How often a held lease is renewed while its work runs.
///
/// Must be comfortably shorter than [leaseTtl] — a third of it by default —
/// so a single missed renewal does not lapse the lease. The gap is the margin
/// that absorbs a slow store, a GC pause or a moment of clock skew.

/// Runs currently dispatched: run id to the attempt in progress.
///
/// The in-process view of what the store has leased to us. Keyed by run so a
/// completion removes exactly its own entry; the entry names the job so the
/// [TomJobDefinition.allowOverlap] check is a lookup rather than a scan, and
/// carries the attempt's future so [drain] can wait for it.
///
/// The future is a completer's rather than [_runOne]'s own, because
/// [_runOne] removes its entry from this map on the way out: reading the
/// future back out of the map to await it would be a race with the removal
/// that ends it. A completer is written once, at dispatch, and completed once,
/// after the run is finished and its entry gone.

/// Coordination leases currently held, keyed by run id.
///
/// Exposed through [heldLeases] so an operator can ask what this node is
/// sitting on without going to the substrate.

/// Jobs already reported as never firing.
///
/// The condition is detected on the planning pass, which runs every tick, and
/// it cannot resolve itself — so without this the alert would repeat for the
/// life of the process. Cleared whenever the registry entry changes, so a
/// corrected job that is still wrong reports again.

/// Catch-up enqueues still allowed in the pass currently running.
///
/// Reset to [maxCatchUpPerTick] at the top of every [tick], and shared across
/// jobs — a budget spent by one job's backlog is a budget the next job's
/// backlog does not get, which is the whole point.

libraries.documentation:
/// The durable job queue — pending and in-flight runs that survive a restart.
///
/// A scheduler that keeps its plan in memory forgets everything the moment the
/// process exits: work that was queued is lost, and work that was *half done*
/// is lost twice over, because nothing records that it was ever started. This
/// library is the store that stops both.
///
/// ## What is persisted, and why that is the whole model
///
/// The unit of persistence is the [TomJobRun] — **one attempt-carrying record
/// per schedule window**. Two fields on it do most of the work:
///
/// * [TomJobRun.scheduledFor] is the *window* the run belongs to, and it is the
///   idempotence key. The scheduler enqueues by window, so replaying a tick —
///   or restarting mid-tick — cannot produce a second run for the same window.
/// * [TomJobRun.runAt] is when the run should next be *attempted*. A retry is
///   therefore not a new record and not a new state; it is the same run with a
///   higher [TomJobRun.attempt] and a later [runAt]. That keeps the state
///   machine at four values and makes "what is outstanding" a single query.
///
/// A claimed run carries [TomJobRun.claimedBy] and [TomJobRun.claimedAt] — a
/// lease. If the process dies holding it, the record stays `running` with a
/// stale timestamp, and [TomJobStore.recoverAbandoned] hands it back. That is
/// what "in-flight runs survive a restart" means concretely: not that the work
/// continues, but that it is visibly outstanding again rather than silently
/// gone.
///
/// ## Implementations
///
/// * [TomMemoryJobStore] — no durability. Correct for tests and for jobs whose
///   loss on restart is acceptable.
/// * [TomFileJobStore] — mirrors the queue to a JSON file on every mutation and
///   reloads it on construction, following the same pattern as the kernel's
///   file-backed log replay store. This is what makes the scheduler durable
///   **without a database**, which matters because `tom_core_kernel` has no
///   persistence layer and must not grow one for this.
///
/// A relational implementation belongs in `tom_core_server`, over the same
/// [TomJobStore] contract; the lease fields are already shaped for the
/// multi-node lock that will need it.

classes.documentation:
/// One attempt-carrying occurrence of a job.
///
/// Mutable by design: a run is a record that moves through states, and copying
/// it on each transition would leave the store deciding which copy is current.
/// All mutation goes through [TomJobStore], so the persisted mirror can never
/// drift from the in-memory one.

constructors.documentation:
/// Decodes a map produced by [toJson].
///
/// An unrecognised state decodes as [TomJobRunState.pending] rather than
/// throwing: a store written by a newer build must not make an older one
/// unable to start, and "pending" is the safe reading — the run is offered
/// again instead of being silently treated as done.

fields.documentation:
/// The schedule window this run realises — the idempotence key.
///
/// For a catch-up run this is the window that was *missed*, not the moment
/// the catch-up was noticed, so a job body can tell which period it is
/// processing.

/// When this run should next be attempted.
///
/// Equal to [scheduledFor] for a first attempt, and the backoff deadline for
/// every retry after that.

/// When the lease was last heard from, `null` when unclaimed. Compared
/// against the lease timeout by [TomJobStore.recoverAbandoned].
///
/// This is the *heartbeat*, not the claim's identity:
/// [TomJobStore.renewClaim] pushes it forward for as long as the attempt
/// runs. Which claim is current is [claimToken]'s job.

/// Which claim of this run is current: strictly increasing across claims,
/// never decreasing, and never reset.
///
/// The fencing token of [TomJobClaim], and the counterpart of `TomLease.token`
/// on the coordination lock — the same problem, solved the same way, because
/// the two must agree about who holds a run. It starts at 0 on an unclaimed
/// run and is incremented by [TomJobStore.claimDue] on every claim, including
/// a re-claim by the node that held it before.
///
/// It is a separate field from [claimedAt] because the two answer different
/// questions and only one of them may move during an attempt. A heartbeat
/// that doubled as the identity would invalidate the claim it was proving
/// alive.

/// When the body actually began executing, `null` if it never did.
///
/// The distinction between "claimed" and "started" is what makes recovery
/// safe to reason about. A `running` run with no [startedAt] was reserved and
/// then lost — the node died between the claim and the dispatch — so nothing
/// was executed and re-queueing it cannot repeat a side effect. A `running`
/// run *with* [startedAt] was interrupted **mid-execution**, and whether it is
/// safe to run again is a property of the job, not of the store: see
/// [TomJobDefinition.requestsRecovery].
///
/// Quartz draws the same line with two trigger states, `ACQUIRED` and
/// `EXECUTING`. It is one nullable instant here because the instant is also
/// worth having — "how long had it been running when the node died" is the
/// first question asked after an incident.

/// Trigger payload for an event-driven run; `null` for clock-driven ones.
///
/// Restricted to a JSON-encodable value so a durable store can round-trip it.

classes.documentation:
/// A ticket proving that one particular claim of [run] was won.
///
/// [TomJobStore.claimDue] issues one per run it hands over, and every
/// transition out of a claim takes the ticket rather than the run. That is the
/// same arrangement `TomLeaseLock` uses — `acquire` returns a `TomLease` and
/// `renew`/`release` take it back — and it is here for the same reason: a
/// caller cannot record an outcome for work it did not claim, because it has
/// nothing to call the method with.
///
/// ## What the ticket has to identify
///
/// Not the run, and not the node: **this claim of this run**. [owner] alone is
/// not enough, because a node can re-claim a run it had previously lost — and
/// then its first attempt's outcome would be accepted as its second's.
///
/// So the fence is [token], a counter that rises on every claim of the run and
/// never repeats. It is exactly `TomLease.token`, for exactly the same reason:
/// two acquisitions of the same thing have to be orderable, or "the current
/// holder" is not a decidable question. That the queue lacked one while the
/// lock had one is why the two could disagree.
///
/// ## Why not the claim instant
///
/// [TomJobRun.claimedAt] looks like a cheaper answer — it is already stored,
/// and it already distinguishes two claims. But it is the *heartbeat*:
/// [TomJobStore.renewClaim] moves it forward while the attempt runs. A ticket
/// fenced on it would be invalidated by the first heartbeat of the very attempt
/// it certifies, so the claimant would silently lose the right to report its
/// own outcome. The identity must be the thing that does *not* move, which is
/// the whole reason the lock separates `token` from `expiresAt`.

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a ticket for [run], claimed by [owner] at [token].
///
/// Public because implementations construct it and tests need to forge one —
/// a fence that could only be built by the store would be untestable against
/// the case it exists for.

fields.documentation:
/// The claimed run, as the claimant sees it.
///
/// Mutable, and mutated by the transitions below so a caller's copy keeps up
/// with what it just wrote — the ticket is the identity, this is the payload.

/// The runner this claim was leased to.
///
/// Not load-bearing on its own — [token] already separates two claims — but
/// checked alongside it, so a ticket threaded to the wrong store or the wrong
/// node is refused rather than coincidentally accepted.

classes.documentation:
/// Durable storage for job runs.
///
/// Every method is asynchronous so a database-backed implementation fits the
/// same contract without the in-memory one having to pretend to be slow.
///
/// **Claiming must be atomic.** [claimDue] transitions a run to `running` and
/// stamps the lease in one step; an implementation that let two callers observe
/// the same run as claimable would double-fire it. The in-process
/// implementations here are atomic by virtue of Dart's single-threaded event
/// loop — they perform no `await` between selecting and stamping.
///
/// **A transition out of a claim only ever *records* a run; it never creates
/// one, and never overrules another claimant.** [release], [markStarted],
/// [renewClaim], [complete] and [fail] are all no-ops unless the store still
/// holds the run, still holds it as `running`, *and* still holds it under the
/// [TomJobClaim] being presented. Each of the five is reached by a caller
/// holding a ticket it obtained some time earlier, so each can be handed a
/// claim the store has since finished, re-queued, handed to another node via
/// [recoverAbandoned], or dropped entirely via [purgeFinished] — and in every
/// one of those cases the outcome of the attempt this caller is reporting is no
/// longer the run's outcome.
///
/// The two halves of the guard answer two different questions, and only both
/// together are sufficient:
///
///  * **Does the store still hold this run, as `running`?** Without it, writing
///    back would resurrect a purged row — [purgeFinished] is the only thing
///    bounding the store, and a re-inserted run carries a freshly stamped
///    [TomJobRun.completedAt] that restarts its retention clock, so it survives
///    every subsequent cutoff.
///  * **Is it still held under *this* claim?** State alone cannot see the case
///    the lease timeout exists for. A node partitioned for longer than the
///    timeout returns to a run that recovery re-queued and a peer re-claimed:
///    the row exists and is `running`, so a state-only guard passes, and the
///    returning node's outcome silently overwrites an attempt the peer is still
///    executing. The peer then heartbeats a run already marked succeeded, and
///    its own completion no-ops.
///
/// The rule costs nothing when nothing went wrong — a run this node still owns
/// is `running` under its own claim by definition — and it is what makes the
/// five safe to call without first re-reading the store.

methods.documentation:
/// Whether an abandoned [run] may be offered again.
///
/// A run that never started always may — nothing executed, so nothing can
/// repeat. One that was interrupted mid-execution may only when its job says
/// so, which is what [requestsRecovery] answers; a `null` predicate means the
/// caller cannot see the job registry, and re-queueing is then the only
/// answer it can give.
///
/// Shared rather than duplicated because both stores must decide identically
/// — but only the decision is shared. Each store applies the resulting
/// transition in its own idiom: field assignment in memory, a different
/// `UPDATE` in SQL.

/// The JSON text a durable store writes for [run]'s payload, or `null` when
/// there is no payload.
///
/// This is also the contract's definition of "JSON-encodable", and every
/// implementation of [enqueue] calls it — *including the in-memory one*,
/// which has nothing to write. The restriction belongs to the contract rather
/// than to any one medium: a payload only the memory store would accept is a
/// payload that fails on the first deployment that has a file or a database,
/// and it should fail on the developer's machine instead.
///
/// Throws a `schedule.job.payload` [TomScheduleException] naming the job and
/// the offending type. The raw [JsonUnsupportedObjectError] says only that
/// *some* object could not be converted, which is of little use several
/// frames below whichever event handed the payload over.

/// Adds [run] to the queue.
///
/// The caller is responsible for idempotence via [findWindow]; the store
/// stores what it is given.
///
/// Throws a `schedule.job.payload` [TomScheduleException], **before changing
/// the queue**, when [TomJobRun.payload] is not JSON-encodable — see
/// [encodePayload].

/// The run already recorded for [jobName] at [scheduledFor], or `null`.
///
/// This is what makes enqueueing idempotent, and therefore what makes a tick
/// safe to replay after a crash.

/// The latest window ever enqueued for [jobName], or `null` if the job has no
/// history in this store.
///
/// The scheduler uses `null` to mean "first sight of this job" and anchors on
/// the present instead of treating all of history as missed.

/// Atomically claims up to [limit] runs that are due at [now], marking each
/// `running` and leasing it to [owner].
///
/// Returns one [TomJobClaim] per run won — the ticket every transition below
/// requires. When [accept] is supplied, only runs it approves are claimed;
/// the scheduler uses it to enforce per-job overlap policy without a second
/// round-trip.

/// Returns the run held by [claim] to `pending` without charging it an
/// attempt.
///
/// The voluntary counterpart of [recoverAbandoned]: a runner that claimed a
/// run and then found it could not proceed — most usually because a cluster
/// peer holds the coordination lease for it — hands it straight back instead
/// of sitting on the claim until the lease times out.
///
/// The attempt count is deliberately untouched. Nothing was attempted, and
/// charging an attempt would let a busy cluster exhaust a job's retry budget
/// through contention alone.
///
/// A no-op unless the store still holds the run as `running` under [claim] —
/// a completion that raced the release must win, and a run recovery has since
/// handed to a peer must not be pushed back into the queue underneath it.

/// Records that [claim]'s body actually began executing at [now].
///
/// Called once per attempt, immediately before the body is handed to the
/// dispatcher, and it is the only thing that distinguishes an interrupted
/// *execution* from an abandoned *reservation*. Without it every `running` row
/// looks the same after a node dies, and recovery has to guess.
///
/// A no-op unless the store still holds the run as `running` under [claim]:
/// the outcome of a run this node has lost belongs to whoever took it over.
/// Stamping `startedAt` onto it would tell recovery that a *different* node's
/// attempt had begun.

/// Refreshes the lease timestamp on [claim] to [now].
///
/// A long-running attempt must be able to say it is still alive, or
/// [recoverAbandoned] would hand its work to a second runner while the first
/// is still going. This is the store half of that heartbeat; the lock half is
/// `TomLeaseLock.renew`.
///
/// A no-op unless the store still holds the run as `running` under [claim] —
/// a heartbeat for a lease this node no longer owns would keep recovery off a
/// run it has already reassigned.
///
/// The [TomJobClaim] itself does not move: it names the claim that was won,
/// and a renewal extends that claim rather than starting a new one. What
/// advances is the stored lease stamp, [TomJobRun.claimedAt]; the token the
/// ticket carries is untouched, so the claimant keeps the right to record its
/// own outcome for as long as the attempt lasts.

/// Marks [claim]'s run succeeded at [now] and releases its lease.
///
/// A no-op unless the store still holds the run as `running` under [claim] —
/// see the rule on [TomJobStore]. Calling it twice therefore records the
/// *first* completion only; the second does not move [TomJobRun.completedAt]
/// forward, so a duplicate outcome cannot extend the run's retention.

/// Records a failed attempt of [claim]'s run.
///
/// When [retryAt] is non-null the run returns to `pending` with its attempt
/// count incremented and `runAt` set to [retryAt]; when it is `null` the run
/// becomes terminally [TomJobRunState.failed].
///
/// A no-op unless the store still holds the run as `running` under [claim],
/// exactly as [complete] is. The retrying branch has a second reason to
/// check: it increments the attempt count, and charging a retry to a run that
/// now belongs to another node would eat a retry budget this caller is no
/// longer spending.

/// Reclaims runs left `running` by a holder that vanished.
///
/// A lease older than [leaseTimeout] is treated as abandoned. The attempt
/// count is **not** incremented: the run may never have started, and charging
/// it an attempt for the scheduler's own death would let a crash loop consume
/// a job's whole retry budget. Returns every run whose state this call
/// changed, so the caller can alert on each; read [TomJobRun.state] to see
/// which way it went.
///
/// ## Re-queued, or terminally failed
///
/// A run that was claimed but never started ([TomJobRun.startedAt] is `null`)
/// always returns to `pending`, unpenalised: nothing ran, so nothing can
/// repeat.
///
/// A run that *had* started was interrupted mid-execution, and re-running it
/// repeats whatever it had already done. Whether that is safe is a property of
/// the job, so [requestsRecovery] is consulted: it returns `true` for a job
/// that has declared itself recoverable ([TomJobDefinition.requestsRecovery]),
/// and such a run goes back to `pending` like any other. A run whose job says
/// otherwise becomes terminally [TomJobRunState.failed] with an explanatory
/// [TomJobRun.lastError], because silently re-running a non-idempotent job is
/// the worse of the two failures and the operator needs to see the decision.
///
/// Passing `null` — the default — recovers everything, which is what a caller
/// that cannot see the job registry must do. A store cannot invent the answer,
/// and re-queueing is the behaviour every caller had before the distinction
/// existed. `TomScheduler` always supplies the predicate.

/// Removes terminal runs that completed before [before], returning how many
/// were dropped.
///
/// Without this the queue is an append-only log that grows forever. It is a
/// caller-driven operation rather than an automatic one because how much
/// history to keep is a deployment decision, not the store's.

fields.documentation:
/// What an interrupted run's [TomJobRun.lastError] says when its job did not
/// ask to be recovered.
///
/// A constant because it is the operator-facing explanation of a decision the
/// framework made on the job's behalf, and it must read the same whichever
/// store recorded it.

classes.documentation:
/// A [TomJobStore] that keeps everything in memory and nothing across restarts.
///
/// The base for [TomFileJobStore], which adds only the mirroring — so both
/// implementations share one copy of the claim and retry semantics rather than
/// two that can disagree.

methods.documentation:
/// The stored run [claim] still holds, or `null` when it no longer does.
///
/// The whole of the rule documented on [TomJobStore], in one place: the run
/// must exist, be `running`, and be `running` *under this claim*. Written
/// once because five transitions ask the same question, and five copies of a
/// three-part predicate is five chances for one of them to drift.

/// The current runs, without going through the asynchronous contract.
///
/// [TomJobStore] is asynchronous so a database-backed implementation fits it,
/// but these in-process stores hold everything already. A durable subclass
/// needs the contents *during* [onMutated], where awaiting would put the
/// mirror one microtask behind the mutation it is mirroring — and a crash in
/// that gap is exactly the case the mirror exists for.

classes.documentation:
/// A [TomJobStore] that mirrors the queue to a JSON file on every mutation.
///
/// Constructing it reloads whatever the previous process left behind, so a
/// scheduler pointed at the same path resumes with its pending runs intact and
/// its in-flight runs visible as expired leases. That is the whole of the
/// durability requirement met without a database — which matters, because
/// `tom_core_kernel` deliberately has no persistence layer.
///
/// Writes are synchronous, flushed and *atomic*: the new queue is staged in a
/// sibling file and renamed over the target, so an interrupted write leaves the
/// previous queue rather than a truncated one. That is not a refinement but the
/// difference between losing one mutation and losing the whole queue — the file
/// is rewritten whole each time, and [_load] can only discard an array it
/// cannot parse.
///
/// The queue is small (one record per window, purged once terminal), so
/// rewriting it whole is cheaper than the bookkeeping an append-only journal
/// would need to compact.

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a store backed by [path], loading any previously persisted runs.
///
/// Writes to [path] if — and only if — what it finds there is damaged: the
/// unreadable content is moved to [_corruptPath] and whatever could be
/// salvaged is written back. That is the one case where constructing a store
/// has an effect on disk, and it is deliberate. A salvage that lived only in
/// memory would be undone by the next restart, and the original has just been
/// renamed away, so not rewriting would lose the runs a second time.

methods.documentation:
/// Reads runs from [file] if it exists, leaving the queue empty when the file
/// is missing, empty, or not a JSON array.
///
/// A record that fails to decode is skipped rather than aborting the load: a
/// single corrupt entry must not stop the scheduler from starting, since
/// starting is how the rest of the queue gets drained. For the same reason
/// this method never throws — the store is built during startup wiring, so a
/// throw here would turn a past crash into a boot failure, taking down the
/// process the durable queue exists to protect.
///
/// What it does not do is stay quiet. Every unreadable state degrades to
/// "carry on with what could be salvaged, and say what was lost", because an
/// empty queue and a destroyed queue otherwise look identical at startup.

/// Reports the loss described by [detail], moves the damaged file aside to
/// [_corruptPath], and writes whatever was salvaged back to [file].
///
/// Called only after the salvaged runs are already in the queue, since the
/// rewrite persists exactly what [snapshotSync] holds.

/// Logs [message] as an error — the channel an operator actually watches.
///
/// Never throws. The store is constructed during startup wiring, and a broken
/// log output must not be what stops the scheduler from starting: reporting a
/// recoverable loss cannot be allowed to become an unrecoverable one.
///
/// `tomLog` already guards the sink it writes to, so the catch below is not
/// what makes the common case safe. It is here because "loading never throws"
/// is this store's own contract, and resting that on another package's
/// internal `try` would make a refactor there a boot failure here.

/// Writes the current queue to [file], creating parent directories as needed
/// and flushing so the bytes reach disk before returning.
///
/// The write is atomic — see [tomWriteFileAtomically]. It has to be: the
/// queue is rewritten whole on every mutation, and [_load] can do nothing
/// with a truncated array but discard it. An overwrite interrupted by a kill
/// or a full disk would therefore not lose *a* run, it would lose the entire
/// durable queue, silently, at the next start.

enums.documentation:
/// The lifecycle of a single [TomJobRun].
///
/// Deliberately four values. "Failed but will be retried" is **not** among them
/// — that is a `pending` run with a higher attempt count and a later
/// `runAt`, because modelling it as its own state would mean two places
/// answering "is there work outstanding?".

libraries.documentation:
/// The *what* half of a background job: its work body and its operational
/// policy — retry, backoff, timeout, overlap and failure alerting.
///
/// CE-JB declares all four of retry, backoff, timeout and alerting as part of a
/// job's definition. They live here, on the definition, rather than inside the
/// job body for one reason: a policy the body implements is a policy every body
/// implements differently, and the moment two jobs disagree about what "retry
/// three times" means, the declaration in the spec has stopped describing the
/// system. The runtime honours these; the body does the work.
///
/// ## The dispatcher seam
///
/// A job's work body is a [TomCommand] — the same unit the kernel's isolate
/// pool already runs. The scheduler never executes one itself; it hands it to a
/// [TomJobDispatcher]. That seam is what keeps the promise not to build a
/// second execution engine: [TomWorkerPoolJobDispatcher] is a thin adapter over
/// [TomWorkerPool], and the only other implementation,
/// [TomInlineJobDispatcher], exists so tests and single-threaded deployments do
/// not have to spawn isolates to exercise scheduling logic.

classes.documentation:
/// How a failed attempt is retried.
///
/// Backoff is exponential from [initialBackoff] by [multiplier], and never
/// exceeds [maxBackoff] — jitter included. [jitter] spreads the retries of jobs
/// that failed together — without it, a downstream service that dropped every
/// job at once gets them all back simultaneously, which is how a brief outage
/// becomes a long one.
///
/// That pile-up is worst once every job has saturated at the ceiling, which is
/// what a long outage produces, so the curve plateaus a jitter's width below
/// [maxBackoff] rather than clamping the jittered value. See [backoffFor].
///
/// The fields have ranges — `maxAttempts >= 1`, `multiplier >= 1`,
/// `jitter` in `0..1`, a non-negative [initialBackoff] and a positive
/// [maxBackoff] — enforced by [validate] rather than by the constructor, which
/// must stay `const` for annotation use.

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a retry policy.
///
/// The arguments are **not** checked here. This constructor has to stay
/// `const` — `@TomScheduledJob(retry: TomRetryPolicy(…))` is an annotation
/// argument, and Dart requires those to be compile-time constants — and a
/// `const` constructor has no body to throw from. The checks therefore run in
/// [validate], which [TomJobDefinition] calls; see there for why that is the
/// honest boundary rather than merely the available one.

methods.documentation:
/// Throws a `schedule.retry.*` [TomScheduleException] if this policy would
/// misbehave, naming [job] so the report identifies which one to fix.
///
/// Called by [TomJobDefinition], which every policy passes through on its way
/// into a scheduler. That placement is deliberate rather than a workaround
/// for the `const` constructor: an unattached policy governs nothing, so the
/// moment a bad value starts producing wrong answers is the moment a job
/// adopts it — and only then is there a name to put in the message.
///
/// Each rule below rejects a value that is currently accepted and then
/// quietly does the wrong thing, which is the shared reason they are errors
/// and not merely unusual settings.

/// The delay before the attempt following [attempt] (1-based).
///
/// [random] is supplied by the scheduler so jitter is reproducible in tests;
/// omitting it yields the exact, unjittered backoff — the low end of the
/// range a jittered call draws from.

fields.documentation:
/// Total attempts including the first, so `1` means "no retry".
///
/// At least 1. `0` is rejected rather than read as "unlimited": it would
/// behave as [none], since [allowsRetryAfter] asks whether the attempt number
/// is below this and no attempt is below zero.

/// The delay before the second attempt.
///
/// May be zero, which retries at once. Never negative.

/// The factor each successive backoff is multiplied by.
///
/// At least 1. Below 1 the "backoff" would shrink on every failure — 10 s,
/// 5 s, 2.5 s — which is the pathology exponential backoff exists to prevent.
/// Exactly 1 is allowed and means a constant delay between attempts.

/// The ceiling backoff never exceeds, [jitter] included.
///
/// Positive, where [initialBackoff] need only be non-negative: retrying at
/// once is a real choice, but a zero ceiling clamps every backoff to nothing
/// and turns [maxAttempts] into a tight spin against whatever just failed.
///
/// Because jitter is added on top, the exponential curve plateaus at
/// `maxBackoff / (1 + jitter)` — with the defaults, 27 min 16 s under the
/// 30 min ceiling — and jitter fills the gap.

/// The fraction of the computed backoff that may be added at random, `0`-`1`.
///
/// Applied additively upward only, so jitter never makes a retry earlier than
/// the backoff the policy promised, and never later than [maxBackoff], whose
/// plateau reserves the room it needs. `0` disables jitter; a negative value
/// is rejected rather than read as "off", since it would silently mean that.

/// The schedule involved, for [TomJobAlertKind.neverFires].
///
/// Carried rather than left to the reader to look up: "job X will never run"
/// is only actionable next to the expression that cannot match.

/// The lease involved, for the coordination kinds.
///
/// For [TomJobAlertKind.leaseStolen] this is the lease *taken over*, so it
/// names the holder that vanished. For [TomJobAlertKind.leaseLost] it is the
/// lease this node held and could no longer renew. For
/// [TomJobAlertKind.lockContended] it is the peer's lease when the lock could
/// report one.

classes.documentation:
/// Where a job's work body is actually run.
///
/// The scheduler owns *when* work runs; a dispatcher owns *where*. Keeping them
/// apart is what lets the scheduler be tested against wall-clock-free fakes
/// while production runs the identical logic over the isolate pool.

methods.documentation:
/// Runs [command] and completes with its result.
///
/// Errors propagate: the scheduler turns them into retries and alerts, so a
/// dispatcher must not swallow them.

classes.documentation:
/// Runs job bodies on an existing [TomWorkerPool].
///
/// This is the production dispatcher, and it is deliberately trivial: the pool
/// already does worker liveness, replacement and back-pressure, so the adapter
/// adds nothing but the call. Anything more here would be the second execution
/// engine this design exists to avoid.

fields.documentation:
/// Whether [close] should also close [pool].
///
/// Defaults to `false`: a pool shared with the rest of the application must
/// not be torn down because the scheduler stopped.

classes.documentation:
/// Runs job bodies in the calling isolate.
///
/// For tests, and for deployments whose jobs are light enough that an isolate
/// hop costs more than the work. Note that a long body will block the event
/// loop — including the scheduler's own tick timer — which is precisely why the
/// pool-backed dispatcher is the default choice in production.

fields.documentation:
/// Produces the work body for one run.
///
/// A **factory**, not a single instance, for two reasons: a command may carry
/// per-run state, and a command dispatched to an isolate is copied, so
/// reusing one instance across runs invites the illusion that mutations to it
/// are visible back on the scheduler side.
///
/// The run is passed in so a body can read its window
/// ([TomJobRun.scheduledFor]), its attempt number, or an event payload.

/// How long one attempt may take before it is treated as failed.
///
/// A timed-out attempt is failed and retried per [retryPolicy]. The work
/// itself is **not** cancelled — an isolate command cannot be interrupted
/// from outside, and killing the worker would take unrelated work with it.
/// Worker liveness stays the pool's concern; a body that can overrun must be
/// written to tolerate a later attempt overlapping it, or must set
/// [allowOverlap] to `false` (the default) so the scheduler holds the next
/// window back.

/// Whether a new run may start while a previous one is still in flight.
///
/// `false` by default, which is the safe reading for the common case of a job
/// that reads and rewrites the same rows. This is *single-node* serialisation
/// only — it holds within one scheduler, not across a cluster.

/// Whether a run interrupted **mid-execution** may be re-queued.
///
/// `false` by default, which is the safe reading: a body that died halfway
/// through may have written half its effects, and re-running it repeats them.
/// Set it to `true` only for a body that is idempotent, or whose work is
/// worthless unless completed — a report regenerated from scratch, a sync that
/// recomputes the whole delta.
///
/// This governs *only* the interrupted case. A run that was claimed and never
/// started is always re-queued regardless, because nothing executed and
/// nothing can therefore repeat; [TomJobRun.startedAt] is what tells the two
/// apart.
///
/// Quartz spells the same opt-in `REQUESTS_RECOVERY` and defaults it off for
/// the same reason.

enums.documentation:
/// What a job does about windows that elapsed while the scheduler was down.
///
/// This has to be *stated*, because every possible behaviour is wrong for some
/// job and there is no default that is merely technical. A nightly report that
/// missed three nights probably wants one catch-up run, not three; a billing
/// job that missed three days probably wants all three; a cache warm-up that
/// missed three days wants none of them.

values.documentation:
/// Ignore every missed window; resume at the next future one.
///
/// Right for work whose value is entirely in being current — cache warming,
/// health probes, anything where a late run does the same thing as the next
/// on-time run and merely costs more.

/// Run the most recent missed window once, and ignore the rest.
///
/// The default, because it preserves the property most schedules are actually
/// asserting — "this ran today" — without replaying an outage's worth of
/// windows. The run carries the missed window in
/// [TomJobRun.scheduledFor], so a body that cares can see it was late.

/// Run every missed window, most recent first, up to
/// [TomJobDefinition.catchUpLimit].
///
/// Right when each window has its own work — a per-period aggregation where
/// skipping a window means that period is never processed. The limit is not
/// optional: an unbounded catch-up after a long outage is a self-inflicted
/// denial of service.

/// A run that was interrupted mid-execution was **not** re-queued, because its
/// job does not set [TomJobDefinition.requestsRecovery].
///
/// The counterpart to [runAbandoned], and separate from it on purpose: those
/// two outcomes look alike in a store — an expired lease either way — but they
/// mean opposite things to an operator. [runAbandoned] says "it will run
/// again"; this says "it will not, and if that work mattered somebody has to
/// decide what to do about it". The run is left terminally `failed` with
/// [TomJobStore.recoveryDeclinedError] recorded against it.

/// A registered job's schedule will never produce a window.
///
/// The quiet misconfiguration: a job that can never run looks exactly like a
/// job that is merely not due yet, and without this nothing would ever say
/// otherwise. It arises two ways — a recurrence that cannot match (`0 0 30 2
/// *` is February 30th) and, more usefully, a `TomOnceSchedule` whose instant
/// had already passed when the job was first registered, which is what a
/// deadline job restored at start-up after a long outage looks like.
///
/// Raised once per job per scheduler rather than once per tick: the condition
/// cannot resolve itself, and an alert that repeats every tick forever is how
/// operators learn to filter a channel.

/// A cluster peer held the coordination lease, so this node did not run the
/// work.
///
/// Routine in a healthy cluster — exactly one node wins each run and the rest
/// report this — so the default sink logs it at debug level. It earns its
/// place as an alert because a job that *never* runs anywhere looks identical
/// to a job nobody scheduled until you can see that every node is conceding.

/// This node took over a lease whose holder had let it expire.
///
/// The cluster-visible answer to "what happened to a run whose holder
/// vanished": the run was picked up here, and the previous holder is named in
/// [TomJobAlert.lease].

/// A lease could not be renewed while its work was still running.
///
/// The serious one. This node no longer owns the run, another node may
/// already be executing it, and the work in flight here cannot be recalled.
/// See `doc/scheduling/scheduling.md` — this is the window in which the
/// at-least-once guarantee becomes visible.

mixins.documentation:
/// Marks an error as one that retrying cannot fix.
///
/// A [TomRetryPolicy] answers "how many times", but some failures should not be
/// attempted a second time at all: a malformed address, a rejected credential,
/// a request the far side answered with "never". Retrying those burns the whole
/// budget on an outcome that was decided at the first attempt, and — worse —
/// delays the *report* of a failure the caller could have acted on immediately.
///
/// The scheduler already draws this line once: a body factory that throws is
/// failed outright rather than retried, because it would throw identically next
/// time. This mixin extends the same judgement to the body itself, which is the
/// only party that can tell a refusal from an outage.
///
/// Mix it into an existing exception to classify it, or throw
/// [TomPermanentJobFailure] when there is no type to reuse:
///
/// ```dart
/// class MailRejected extends TomException with TomPermanentFailure { … }
/// ```
///
/// The default remains *transient*. An unrecognised error is retried, because
/// retrying a permanent failure costs a few wasted attempts whereas dropping a
/// transient one loses the work.

typeAliases.documentation:
/// Where alerts are delivered.
///
/// A plain function so a deployment can route alerts anywhere — a log, a
/// notification service, a metrics counter — without the scheduler knowing
/// about any of them.

libraries.documentation:
/// Cluster coordination — the lease-based lock that makes one scheduled run
/// execute once across a server cluster.
///
/// The single-node scheduler serialises a job's runs with an in-process set;
/// that set is invisible to the machine next to it. Point two schedulers at one
/// shared queue and both will claim the same due run, because nothing arbitrates
/// between them. This library is that arbiter.
///
/// ## What a lease is, and why not a plain lock
///
/// A plain mutual-exclusion lock has one fatal property in a distributed system:
/// if the holder dies, the lock is held forever. Every distributed lock is
/// therefore a **lease** — an acquisition with an expiry, so a holder that
/// vanishes stops being a holder without having to do anything. That trade is
/// not free: it converts "held forever" into "may be released while the holder
/// still thinks it holds it", which is the failure this library is careful about
/// rather than one it pretends away.
///
/// Three mechanisms manage that trade:
///
/// * **TTL** — a lease expires at [TomLease.expiresAt] whether or not the holder
///   is alive. This is what makes holder death self-healing.
/// * **Renewal** — a live holder pushes its expiry forward while it works, so a
///   job legitimately running longer than the TTL is not double-fired. Without
///   renewal the TTL would have to exceed the slowest possible run, which means
///   a dead holder blocks the resource for that long too. Renewal is what lets
///   the TTL be short *and* long jobs be safe.
/// * **Fencing tokens** — see below.
///
/// ## Fencing tokens: why the guarantee is what it is
///
/// A lease cannot, by itself, guarantee that two holders never overlap. The
/// holder might be paused — a stop-the-world GC, a hypervisor suspend, a stalled
/// disk — for longer than its TTL, wake up believing it still holds the lease,
/// and write. Meanwhile the lease expired and somebody else acquired it. No
/// amount of clock care fixes this, because the pause happens *between* the
/// holder's check and its write.
///
/// The standard answer is a **fencing token**: every acquisition carries a
/// number ([TomLease.token]) that strictly increases per resource. A protected
/// resource that records the highest token it has seen and rejects anything
/// lower will reject the stale holder's late write. That turns "probably one
/// holder" into a real guarantee — but only for resources that check the token.
///
/// This library therefore issues tokens and states plainly what they buy:
///
/// * Against **any** resource: a lease gives single-active-holder under normal
///   operation (no pause exceeding the TTL). Duplicate execution is possible in
///   the pause case.
/// * Against a **token-checking** resource: the stale holder's writes are
///   rejected, so the effect is single-fire even under pause.
///
/// See `doc/scheduling/scheduling.md` for the execution guarantee CE-JB job
/// bodies must be written against. The short version is that job bodies must be
/// **idempotent**; the lock reduces duplicate work, it does not abolish it.
///
/// ## The substrate
///
/// [TomLeaseLock] is a port, not a single implementation, and the substrate is a
/// deployment choice — with one rule: **the lock must live in the same failure
/// domain as the job queue it guards.** A lock in a different store than the
/// queue can be healthy while the queue is unreachable, at which point a node
/// holds a valid lease over work it cannot see, and no reconciliation exists.
/// So a file-backed queue pairs with a file-backed lock and a database-backed
/// queue pairs with a database-backed lock.
///
/// Two implementations ship here, matching the two [TomJobStore]s:
///
/// * [TomMemoryLeaseLock] — one process. Correct for tests and for single-node
///   deployments, which are the majority and are fully served by it.
/// * [TomFileLeaseLock] — many processes over a shared filesystem, built on
///   atomic exclusive-create and atomic rename.
///
/// A relational implementation belongs in `tom_core_server` alongside the
/// relational queue, over this same contract.

classes.documentation:
/// A granted, time-bounded claim on a named resource.
///
/// Immutable: renewing produces a new lease rather than mutating this one, so a
/// holder can never be confused about which expiry it is working against, and a
/// stale reference stays honestly stale.

fields.documentation:
/// The fencing token: strictly increasing per [resource] across acquisitions.
///
/// Hand this to any resource that supports fencing so it can reject a write
/// from a holder whose lease has since been taken over. A resource that
/// ignores it still benefits from the lease; it just does not get the
/// pause-safety guarantee.

/// When it lapses unless renewed, by the acquiring node's clock.
///
/// Both timestamps are the *holder's* view. Across nodes with skewed clocks
/// these are not directly comparable, which is why [TomFileLeaseLock] compares
/// against the reading node's own clock and why the TTL should comfortably
/// exceed the worst expected skew.

classes.documentation:
/// What a lock currently knows about a resource — the answer to "who holds it,
/// when does it expire, and is that still true?".
///
/// Returned by [TomLeaseLock.inspect] and deliberately readable without
/// acquiring anything, because the first thing an operator does when a job stops
/// running is ask who is sitting on it.

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates an inspection result.
///
/// [lease] is required even though it is nullable, because "never held" has
/// its own constructor below. Defaulting it would give that state a second
/// spelling reachable by omission, and an inspection result that says
/// "never held" because an argument was forgotten is worse than no answer.

fields.documentation:
/// The most recent lease on record, or `null` if the resource was never held.
///
/// Present even when [isHeld] is false: a released or expired lease still
/// answers "who had it last", which is the question worth asking after a node
/// vanished.

/// Whether the recorded lease ended by an orderly [TomLeaseLock.release].
///
/// The distinction that matters after an incident: a lease that was released
/// belonged to a node that shut down cleanly, while a lease that merely
/// expired belonged to one that **vanished**. Both leave `isHeld` false, and
/// telling them apart is the difference between "the cluster scaled down" and
/// "a node died mid-run".

classes.documentation:
/// A lease-based distributed lock.
///
/// Implementations must make [acquire] **atomic**: two callers racing for a free
/// resource must not both receive a lease. That is the entire load-bearing
/// requirement — everything else in this contract is bookkeeping around it.

methods.documentation:
/// Attempts to take [resource] for [owner] until `now + ttl`.
///
/// Returns the granted lease, or `null` when another owner currently holds it.
/// Returning `null` is an ordinary outcome in a cluster — it means another
/// node is doing the work — and is not an error.
///
/// A lease whose expiry has passed is taken over rather than refused: that is
/// how a holder that died stops blocking the resource.

/// Extends [lease] by [ttl] from now.
///
/// Returns the new lease, or `null` if it could no longer be renewed — the
/// resource was taken over while this holder was working. `null` is a serious
/// signal: the caller must assume somebody else is now running the work and
/// must stop treating its own results as authoritative.

/// Releases [lease] if it is still held by its owner.
///
/// Releasing a lease that was already taken over is a no-op rather than an
/// error: the holder losing a race it did not know it was in should not also
/// have to handle an exception on the way out.

/// Discards every record of [resource], fencing-token chain included.
///
/// Only safe for a resource whose name will never appear again — a run id,
/// which is unique for the life of the queue. Forgetting a **stable** name
/// such as a job's restarts its tokens at 1, and a token that repeats is not
/// a fencing token. It exists because a per-run resource would otherwise
/// accumulate one dead record for every window ever executed.

classes.documentation:
/// A [TomLeaseLock] confined to one process.
///
/// Atomic by virtue of Dart's single-threaded event loop: it performs no `await`
/// between reading a record and replacing it, so no other code in this isolate
/// can interleave.
///
/// Correct for tests, and correct in production for a **single-node**
/// deployment, where it upgrades the scheduler's in-process overlap set to a
/// uniform mechanism at no cost. It coordinates nothing across processes, which
/// is the whole reason [TomFileLeaseLock] exists.

fields.documentation:
/// The current record per resource — kept after release rather than removed,
/// exactly as [TomFileLeaseLock] keeps its file, so both implementations can
/// answer "who had it last, and did they leave cleanly?".

classes.documentation:
/// A [TomLeaseLock] that coordinates **separate processes** through a shared
/// directory.
///
/// ## How it is atomic
///
/// One primitive carries the whole design: `File.createSync(exclusive: true)`
/// is `O_EXCL`, so it fails if the path already exists. That is an atomic
/// test-and-set shared by every process on the filesystem. (`Directory
/// .createSync` is **not** usable for this — it succeeds silently on an
/// existing directory, so the classic mkdir-mutex idiom does not port to Dart.)
///
/// The trick is *what* gets created. Each resource owns a directory, and each
/// acquisition writes a file named after its own fencing token:
///
/// ```text
/// <root>/<encoded resource>/3.lease
/// ```
///
/// Taking over a lapsed lease at token 3 means creating `4.lease` exclusively.
/// Every contender computes the same next token, so exactly one create wins and
/// the rest are refused by the kernel. Nothing is ever deleted from under a
/// reader, and — the property that matters — **the resource is never briefly
/// absent**, so no late arrival can mistake a takeover in progress for a free
/// resource and claim it in parallel.
///
/// A design that instead renames the stale record aside and re-creates it has
/// exactly that hole: between the rename and the create the resource looks
/// untaken, and a node arriving in that window wins it legitimately, alongside
/// the node doing the takeover. Versioned names close the window by never
/// opening it.
///
/// Superseded records are pruned once two newer ones exist, so a resource costs
/// two small files however many times it changes hands. Per-*run* resources are
/// dropped outright by [forget], since a run id never recurs.
///
/// ## What it assumes
///
/// A filesystem where exclusive-create and rename are atomic between the
/// processes sharing it. That is true for a local filesystem and for a
/// well-behaved network filesystem, but network filesystems vary and some cache
/// aggressively enough to break it. Where the deployment already has a database,
/// a database-backed lock is the safer substrate; this one exists so that a
/// file-backed queue has a lock in its own failure domain.
///
/// Clocks are compared across nodes only through expiry timestamps, so the TTL
/// must comfortably exceed the fleet's worst clock skew.

methods.documentation:
/// Grants [token] on [resource] to [owner], or `null` if a peer got there.
///
/// The exclusive create *is* the arbitration: every contender computes the
/// same next token, and the kernel picks the one winner.

/// The highest-numbered record for [resource], or `null` if there is none.
///
/// [record] is `null` when that file exists but cannot be read — the
/// mid-create and corrupt cases, which the caller separates by age.

/// Drops records below [oldest], keeping the current one and its predecessor.
///
/// The predecessor is kept deliberately: a reader that listed the directory a
/// moment before a takeover is about to read the version it saw, and pulling
/// that file out from under it would turn a clean "somebody else holds this"
/// into an unreadable record.

/// Whether an unreadable record is old enough that its writer must be gone.
///
/// The window between exclusive-create and the first write is microseconds; a
/// record still unreadable a whole TTL later was left by a process that died
/// inside that window.
///
/// Measured against the **real** clock rather than [clock]: this asks how long
/// a file has physically existed, and a filesystem timestamp is only
/// comparable with wall time. An injected clock would compare a simulated
/// instant with a real one and get an arbitrary answer.

/// The directory holding every record for [resource].
///
/// Resource names come from job names and run ids, which may contain
/// characters a filesystem will not take, so the name is encoded rather than
/// used raw. Giving each resource its own directory also keeps [_scan] to a
/// two-entry listing however many resources the lock coordinates.

classes.documentation:
/// The on-disk shape of a lease: the lease itself plus whether it was released.
///
/// "Released" is recorded rather than represented by deleting the file, because
/// the file is also where the fencing token lives and the token must outlive
/// the lease that carried it.

libraries.documentation:
/// Bearer token authentication and principal management.
///
/// This library provides JWT-based bearer token authentication for the
/// Tom framework. It manages authentication and authorization tokens,
/// and provides access to the current user's [TomPrincipal].
///
/// ## Token Types
///
/// The system uses two separate JWT tokens:
/// - **Authentication Token**: Proves user identity
/// - **Authorization Token**: Contains access control information (ACI)
///
/// This separation allows for different token lifetimes and security
/// levels for identity vs. permissions.
///
/// ## Usage
///
/// ```dart
/// // Set tokens after successful login
/// TomBearerAuthentication.setTokens(authToken, authzToken);
///
/// // Access current user information
/// var principal = TomBearerAuthentication.getPrincipal();
/// print('Logged in as: ${principal.username}');
/// print('Roles: ${principal.aci.roles}');
/// ```
///
/// ## Token Payload Structure
///
/// Public payload (visible in JWT):
/// - User info: username, firstname, lastname, email, etc.
/// - Context: organization, application, process
/// - Localization: language, country, timezone
///
/// Secret payload (encrypted):
/// - Security: roles, groups, entitlements, resourceKeys
/// - Session context
///
/// See also:
/// - [TomAuthenticationMessage] for login requests
/// - [TomPrincipal] for the user context object
/// - [TomAccessControlInformation] for the ACI structure

classes.documentation:
/// Manages JWT bearer token authentication.
///
/// This singleton class stores and manages authentication/authorization
/// tokens, and provides the current user's [TomPrincipal].
///
/// ## Setting Tokens
///
/// After successful authentication, store the tokens:
///
/// ```dart
/// TomBearerAuthentication.setTokens(
///   result.authenticationToken,
///   result.authorizationToken,
/// );
/// ```
///
/// ## Getting the Principal
///
/// Access the current authenticated user:
///
/// ```dart
/// var principal = TomBearerAuthentication.getPrincipal();
/// if (principal.authenticated) {
///   print('Welcome, ${principal.user.firstname}!');
/// }
/// ```
///
/// ## Token Conversion
///
/// For server-side use, convert a principal back to tokens:
///
/// ```dart
/// var (publicPayload, privatePayload) =
///     TomBearerAuthentication.convertPrincipalToTokenPayload(
///       principal,
///       aciDefinition,
///       clientCountry,
///       clientLanguage,
///     );
/// ```

methods.documentation:
/// Sets the authentication and authorization tokens.
///
/// This should be called after successful authentication to store
/// the tokens and make the principal available.
///
/// - [authenticationToken]: JWT proving user identity
/// - [authorizationToken]: JWT containing access rights
/// - [decrypt]: Whether to decrypt the token's secret data (default: true)

/// Discards the stored token pair and the cached principal.
///
/// Use on logout, or when a login was answered with an interim 2FA token the
/// client is abandoning. Leaves the singleton in the same state as a freshly
/// constructed one, so a following [getPrincipal] throws rather than handing
/// back a stale identity.

/// Converts JWT token payloads to a [TomPrincipal] object.
///
/// Extracts user information, context, and access control data from
/// the token payloads to construct a complete principal.
///
/// - [authenticationToken]: The parsed authentication JWT
/// - [authorizationToken]: Optional parsed authorization JWT
///   (uses authentication token if not provided)
///
/// Returns a [TomPrincipal] with all extracted information.

/// Gets the current authenticated principal.
///
/// Returns the [TomPrincipal] for the current session. The principal
/// is cached after first creation for performance.
///
/// Throws [StateError] if no tokens have been set or if the tokens
/// cannot be parsed into a valid principal.
///
/// ```dart
/// var principal = TomBearerAuthentication.getPrincipal();
/// print('User: ${principal.username}');
/// print('Roles: ${principal.aci.roles}');
/// ```

/// Replaces the current principal's access control information.
///
/// Refreshes a user's permissions without a full re-authentication, which is
/// what a periodic poll of the server's authorization state needs: the
/// server holds the whole answer, so the whole answer is what arrives here.
///
/// **Replacement, stated in the name and enforced by the signature.** Every
/// collection is set to exactly what this call is given, so what a caller
/// passes is the set the principal ends up with, never a delta. All five are
/// `required` and named: none can be left out, and none can be transposed
/// with its neighbour — four adjacent `List<String>` parameters
/// distinguished only by position would grant the wrong things at run time
/// while compiling cleanly, and an optional `resourceRoles` would let a
/// caller clear every per-resource grant by saying nothing. Since
/// `TomResourceRoleAdapter` resolves an ungranted resource to
/// `TomAuthState.none`, that omission is invisible: the UI thins out and
/// nothing is raised.
///
/// Pass `const []` or `const {}` deliberately to clear one.
///
/// - [groups]: The group memberships the principal should end up with
/// - [roles]: The roles it should end up with
/// - [entitlements]: The entitlements it should end up with
/// - [resourceKeys]: The resource keys it should end up with
/// - [resourceRoles]: The per-resource role map (`resourceKey → roleName`)

/// Converts a principal to JWT token payloads.
///
/// Creates the public and private payloads needed to generate
/// authentication and authorization JWT tokens.
///
/// - [principal]: The principal to convert
/// - [definition]: Optional ACI definition for resolving full permissions
/// - [clientCountry]: Client's country for localization
/// - [clientLanguage]: Client's language for localization
/// - [challenge]: A pending second-factor challenge to carry in the token,
///   making this an *interim* token rather than a completed session
/// - [twoFactorVerification]: The mechanism's server-only verification
///   payload, placed in the encrypted section (only meaningful with
///   [challenge])
/// - [twoFactorSatisfied]: Set on the second pass, once the factor has been
///   verified, to clear the requirement the principal still carries
///
/// ## Which principal decides redaction
///
/// Eleven identity fields are filtered through
/// [TomResourceKeyProtection.protectFor] under the key `tom.user.<field>`,
/// and the operand is **[principal] itself** — the subject the token is being
/// minted for — not whoever the ambient zone names. A field survives when the
/// subject's own ACI holds its key, and falls back to `null` (or `const {}`
/// for `attributes`) when it does not.
///
/// The distinction is load-bearing on the login path, which is where this is
/// called from: the subject is being authenticated at that moment, so nothing
/// is installed on the zone yet, and reading the zone would be fail-closed —
/// with [TomResourceKeyProtection.globalSettingProtectionDefault] on, every
/// protected field in every minted token would come back empty.
///
/// `username`, `userId` and `userTimezone` are not filtered and always travel
/// in the clear.
///
/// ## The `requires2FA` claim
///
/// The claim is **fail-safe**: it is true whenever a [challenge] is being
/// issued *or* the principal declares it needs a second factor, and only
/// [twoFactorSatisfied] clears it. So a caller that mints tokens without
/// going through the two-pass flow cannot accidentally hand a 2FA user a
/// fully-authenticated token by forgetting a parameter — the omission
/// produces an unusable interim token, not an over-privileged one.
///
/// Returns a tuple of (publicPayload, privatePayload):
/// - Public payload: User info visible in the JWT
/// - Private payload: Encrypted security information (ACI)
///
/// ```dart
/// var (publicPayload, privatePayload) =
///     TomBearerAuthentication.convertPrincipalToTokenPayload(
///       principal,
///       aciDefinition,
///       'US',
///       'en',
///     );
/// var token = TomServerJwtToken.create(publicPayload, privatePayload);
/// ```

fields.documentation:
/// Global singleton instance for bearer authentication.
///
/// Assignable on purpose, and not `final`: every static accessor on this
/// class — [token], [getPrincipal], [setTokens] and the rest — reads through
/// this field, so replacing it swaps the whole of the cached client-side
/// authentication state in one move. That is the isolation seam tests use,
/// in `setUp`/`tearDown`, to stop one test's login leaking into the next.

libraries.documentation:
/// Access control system for the Tom framework.
///
/// This library provides a comprehensive access control system that enables
/// fine-grained authorization for resources, endpoints, and data fields.
/// It supports multiple authorization paradigms including:
///
/// - **Role-based access control (RBAC)**: Grant access based on user roles
/// - **Group-based access control**: Grant access based on group membership
/// - **Entitlement-based access control**: Grant access based on entitlements
/// - **Resource key protection**: Protect individual data fields or resources
/// - **Custom access handlers**: Implement custom authorization logic
///
/// ## Usage Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Check if a resource is accessible
/// if (!tomProtected('user.email')) {
///   displayEmail(user.email);
/// }
///
/// // Use access control annotations on endpoints
/// @TomRoleAccess(['admin', 'manager'])
/// void deleteUser(int userId) { ... }
///
/// // Use entitlement-based access
/// @TomEntitlementAccess(['feature.premium.*'])
/// void runPremiumFeature() { ... }
/// ```
///
/// See also:
/// - [TomPrincipal] for the authenticated user context
/// - [TomAccessControlInformation] for the user's access rights

classes.documentation:
/// Provides resource-level protection based on resource keys.
///
/// This class implements a protection mechanism that can selectively hide
/// or expose data based on the current user's access rights. It integrates
/// with the zone-based [TomPrincipal] to determine accessibility.
///
/// ## Global Settings
///
/// The protection behavior can be controlled through global settings:
/// - [globalSettingProtectionDefault]: Master switch for protection
/// - [globalSettingUseAutomaticEndpointProtection]: Auto-protect API endpoints
/// - [globalSettingUseAutomaticJsonProtection]: Auto-protect JSON serialization
/// - [globalSettingUseAutomaticDatabaseProtection]: Auto-protect database queries
/// - [globalSettingUseAutomaticUIProtection]: Whether the Flutter toolkit's
///   four-state authorization model applies at all
///
/// Two of the four are honoured: [globalSettingUseAutomaticEndpointProtection]
/// by the server's endpoint pipeline, and [globalSettingUseAutomaticUIProtection]
/// by `tom_flutter_ui`'s `TomAuthLayers`. The remaining two —
/// [globalSettingUseAutomaticJsonProtection] and
/// [globalSettingUseAutomaticDatabaseProtection] — name tiers that do not exist
/// yet and are read by nothing; each says so on its own declaration, and until
/// a tier is built, the protection at that layer is whatever the deployment
/// writes by hand with [protect].
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Enable protection globally
/// TomResourceKeyProtection.globalSettingProtectionDefault = true;
///
/// // Protect a field - returns fallback if user lacks access
/// var email = TomResourceKeyProtection.protect(
///   user.email,
///   '[hidden]',
///   'user.email',
/// );
/// ```

methods.documentation:
/// Protects a value based on [principal]'s resource key access.
///
/// Returns [data] if [principal] holds [resourceKey], otherwise [fallback].
/// A `null` principal holds no keys, so it gets [fallback].
///
/// This is [protect] stated over an explicit operand, and it is the form to
/// reach for whenever a principal is already in hand — reading the zone when
/// the caller named someone answers a different question. It matters most
/// where the two differ irreconcilably: minting a token for a principal
/// *being authenticated* means there is no principal on the zone yet, so the
/// zone-reading form is fail-closed and blanks every protected field.
///
/// **Requires [globalSettingProtectionDefault] to be `true`.** It defaults to
/// `false`, and while it is off this returns [data] for every key and every
/// principal — so a call added for protection protects nothing until the
/// switch is set.
///
/// - [principal]: The principal whose keys decide access, or `null`
/// - [data]: The actual value to return if access is granted
/// - [fallback]: The value to return if access is denied
/// - [resourceKey]: The key identifying the protected resource

/// Protects a value based on the current user's resource key access.
///
/// Returns [data] if the current user — the principal installed on the
/// ambient zone — has access to [resourceKey], otherwise returns [fallback].
/// Where a principal is already in hand, call [protectFor] instead.
///
/// **Requires [globalSettingProtectionDefault] to be `true`.** It defaults to
/// `false`, and while it is off this returns [data] for every key and every
/// principal — so a call added for protection protects nothing until the
/// switch is set.
///
/// - [data]: The actual value to return if access is granted
/// - [fallback]: The value to return if access is denied
/// - [resourceKey]: The key identifying the protected resource

/// Checks if a resource is protected from [principal].
///
/// Returns `true` if the resource is protected (the principal lacks access),
/// `false` if the resource is accessible. A `null` [principal] is treated as
/// holding no keys, so a protected resource is out of its reach.
///
/// Gated on [globalSettingProtectionDefault]: while that is `false` — its
/// default — this answers `false` for every key and every principal, before
/// any key is consulted.
///
/// This is the rule itself, stated over an explicit operand. [isProtected]
/// applies it to whoever the ambient zone names; [TomResourceKeyAccess]
/// applies it to the principal its caller asked about.
///
/// - [principal]: The principal whose keys decide access, or `null`
/// - [resourceKey]: The key identifying the resource to check

/// Checks if a resource is protected from the current user.
///
/// The "current user" is the principal installed on the ambient zone. Where
/// a principal is already in hand, call [isProtectedFor] instead — reading
/// the zone when the caller named someone answers a different question.
///
/// Returns `true` if the resource is protected (user lacks access),
/// `false` if the resource is accessible.
///
/// Gated on [globalSettingProtectionDefault] exactly as [isProtectedFor] is.
/// Note the two ways this answers `false`: protection switched off (nothing
/// is protected) and the principal holding the key. Outside a request zone
/// there is no principal, so with protection on this answers `true` for
/// everything — fail-closed.
///
/// - [resourceKey]: The key identifying the resource to check

fields.documentation:
/// Master switch to enable/disable resource protection globally.
///
/// When `false`, all resources are accessible. When `true`, resource
/// access is evaluated based on the current principal's resource keys.

/// When enabled, API endpoints are automatically protected based on metadata.
///
/// The one automatic switch that is honoured: `tom_core_server`'s endpoint
/// pipeline consults it when the ordinary access check has already refused.

/// Whether `tom_flutter_ui`'s four-state authorization model applies.
///
/// `true` by default and normally left there: with it on, every element
/// resolves through `TomAuthLayers` — resource, group, enclosing ceiling,
/// authorizer, UI-state controller — and renders at the state that
/// composition produces.
///
/// Setting it `false` **bypasses the model and grants every UI resource it
/// protects**. Build-time UI state still applies, because a controller that
/// says "this form is in view mode" is describing the screen rather than the
/// principal. This is a diagnostic switch: it answers "is this element hidden
/// because of authorization, or because of something else?" in one step,
/// without editing grants. It is not a deployment setting, and a server that
/// relies on it for anything is relying on the client to police itself.
///
/// It replaces three earlier switches — for form fields, for actions, and for
/// disabling rather than hiding an action — that described distinctions the
/// four-state model already draws: hidden, read-only and disabled are three
/// of its four states, resolved per element rather than per category.

/// When enabled, JSON serialization would automatically respect resource
/// protection.
///
/// **No tier implements this.** Nothing reads the flag; the `json` module
/// does not reference resource-key protection at all, so setting it changes
/// nothing. Serialization protects what the caller protects explicitly with
/// [protect] or [protectFor], and nothing else.

/// When enabled, database queries would automatically filter protected
/// resources.
///
/// **No tier implements this.** Nothing reads the flag; `object_persistence`
/// does not reference resource-key protection, so setting it changes
/// nothing. Row-level filtering that *is* implemented lives in the server's
/// data-scope layer (`TomDataScope` and its sources), which is a separate
/// mechanism keyed on scopes rather than on resource keys.

classes.documentation:
/// Abstract base class for all access control types.
///
/// Extend this class to implement custom access control logic.
/// All access control implementations must override [checkPrincipalAccess]
/// to determine if a [TomPrincipal] should have access.
///
/// ## Built-in Implementations
///
/// - [TomNoAccess]: Denies all access
/// - [TomPublicAccess]: Allows all access
/// - [TomAuthenticatedAccess]: Requires authenticated user
/// - [TomGuestAccess]: Allows guests and authenticated users
/// - [TomRoleAccess]: Requires specific roles
/// - [TomGroupAccess]: Requires group membership
/// - [TomEntitlementAccess]: Requires specific entitlements
/// - [TomResourceKeyAccess]: Checks resource key protection
/// - [TomCustomAccess]: Delegates to custom handler
/// - [TomGradedAccess]: Composes three of the above into all four
///   [TomAuthState] values — the only built-in that can resolve to
///   [TomAuthState.disabled] or [TomAuthState.read]
///
/// ## Two operands: the user and the deployment
///
/// Evaluation has **two** operands, not one. A user's grant is met with the
/// grant of the deployment the code is running in — the *ambient server
/// principal* ([TomServerPrincipal]) — and the effective answer is the more
/// restrictive of the two: `min(user, server)` over the four-state ladder,
/// binary AND for two-state controls. A user's [TomAuthState.full] on a feature
/// this deployment is not entitled to evaluates to [TomAuthState.none]. The
/// deployment's grants cap every user's grants; they never widen them.
///
/// ## What subclasses implement
///
/// The public entry points [checkAccessibility] and [resolveAuthState] compute
/// that meet and are **not overridable** — a cap that any subclass could
/// sidestep by overriding the method everybody already calls would not be a cap
/// at all. Subclasses instead implement the two hooks:
///
/// - [checkPrincipalAccess] / [resolvePrincipalAuthState] — the *user* operand:
///   what this control demands of the calling principal. This is the whole of
///   a subclass's own rule; nothing about the deployment belongs here.
/// - [resolveServerAuthState] — the *deployment* operand: what grade this
///   control's subject is granted to the deployment. It defaults to
///   [TomAuthState.full] (uncapped), because most controls name a *user*
///   requirement (a role, a group, a resource key) and say nothing about
///   deployment features. [TomEntitlementAccess] is the grant carrier that
///   actually answers it.

methods.documentation:
/// The **user** operand: whether [principal] satisfies this control.
///
/// Returns `true` if the principal's own grants suffice, `false` otherwise.
/// A `null` principal typically represents an unauthenticated user.
///
/// This answer is deliberately *un*capped — it is one half of the meet the
/// public [checkAccessibility] computes. Consumers want that public method;
/// this one exists for subclasses to implement and for composite controls
/// (like [TomGradedAccess]) to interrogate their children without the cap
/// being applied once per nesting level.

/// The **user** operand resolved to a four-state [TomAuthState].
///
/// The default bridges the binary [checkPrincipalAccess] onto two states —
/// granted → [TomAuthState.full], denied → [TomAuthState.none] — so every
/// binary access control (role/group/entitlement/etc.) keeps its meaning
/// while gaining a four-state outcome. Controls that need to express
/// [TomAuthState.read] (read-only) or [TomAuthState.disabled] (visible but
/// locked) override this; see [TomGradedAccess].

/// The **deployment** operand: the highest grade this control's subject is
/// granted to the deployment identified by [serverPrincipal].
///
/// Defaults to [TomAuthState.full] — *uncapped*. That default is the correct
/// one rather than a lenient shortcut: a control that names a user role, a
/// group or a resource key expresses nothing about which **features** a
/// deployment licenses, so it has no opinion to contribute to the meet, and a
/// participant with no opinion must return the identity element. Returning
/// anything lower would make an unrelated deployment grant silently veto a
/// role check.
///
/// [TomEntitlementAccess] overrides this — entitlement patterns *are* the
/// grant carrier — and [TomGradedAccess] composes its children's answers into
/// the ladder.

/// Resolves the **effective** four-state authorization for [principal]: the
/// user's grade met with the deployment's.
///
/// When no server principal is installed the deployment has no identity to
/// cap with, and the result is the user's grade alone — byte-for-byte the
/// behaviour of every deployment built before server-as-principal existed.
/// This is why the guard tests [TomServerPrincipal.current] for `null` rather
/// than evaluating against an empty principal: an empty entitlement set means
/// "entitled to nothing" and would deny everything.

/// Whether [principal] may see this control's content, deployment cap
/// included.
///
/// Defined as [TomAuthState.showsContent] over [resolveAuthState], so the
/// binary and graded views can never disagree. For a two-state control this
/// is exactly the plain AND of the user's grant and the deployment's, which
/// is what the meet degenerates to when neither operand can express the
/// middle states.

classes.documentation:
/// Access control that resolves to **all four** [TomAuthState] values by
/// composing three binary controls — one each named after the state it grants:
/// [full], [read], and [disabled].
///
/// Resolution, from most to least privileged:
///
/// - [full] grants → [TomAuthState.full] (interactive/editable)
/// - else [read] grants → [TomAuthState.read] (value visible, not editable)
/// - else [disabled] grants → [TomAuthState.disabled] (rendered but locked)
/// - else → [TomAuthState.none] (hidden)
///
/// When [read] is omitted it defaults to [full]; when [disabled] is omitted it
/// defaults to [read]. This preserves the natural monotonicity that anyone who
/// can edit can also read and see, so the common case only needs a single
/// control.
///
/// For binary consumers, [checkAccessibility] collapses to "can see content"
/// ([TomAuthState.showsContent]): a [TomAuthState.read] or [TomAuthState.full]
/// outcome is accessible; [TomAuthState.disabled] and [TomAuthState.none] are
/// not.
///
/// ```dart
/// final salary = TomGradedAccess(
///   full: const TomRoleAccess(['payroll.admin']),
///   read: const TomRoleAccess(['manager']),
///   disabled: const TomAuthenticatedAccess(),
/// );
/// final state = salary.resolveAuthState(currentPrincipal);
/// // payroll.admin → full, manager → read, other staff → disabled,
/// // anonymous → none.
/// ```

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a graded access control.
///
/// Only [full] is required; [read] defaults to [full] and [disabled]
/// defaults to [read].

methods.documentation:
/// The deployment's grade for this graded control: the same ladder, walked
/// against the *server's* grants instead of the user's.
///
/// A tier counts as granted to the deployment when its child reports itself
/// fully granted. So a deployment entitled to a feature's view tier but not
/// its edit tier yields [TomAuthState.read], which caps even an editing user
/// down to read — the worked example in `codespecs_mapping.md` §5.26,
/// falling straight out of the structure rather than out of a special case.
///
/// Note the asymmetry with [resolvePrincipalAuthState]: children that express
/// no deployment opinion answer [TomAuthState.full] (the identity element),
/// so a graded tree of pure role checks resolves to `full` here and caps
/// nothing.

classes.documentation:
/// Denies access to all users.
///
/// Use this to temporarily disable a feature or endpoint without
/// removing the code. Useful for maintenance or staged rollouts.
///
/// ```dart
/// @TomNoAccess()
/// void deprecatedEndpoint() { ... }
/// ```

/// Allows access to all users, including unauthenticated ones.
///
/// Use this for public endpoints that don't require any authentication.
///
/// ```dart
/// @TomPublicAccess()
/// String getPublicInfo() => 'Hello, World!';
/// ```

/// Requires a fully authenticated user (not a guest).
///
/// Use this for endpoints that require a logged-in user with
