libraries.documentation:
/// Exception base classes and error handling utilities.
///
/// This library provides the foundation for exception handling throughout
/// the tom_core framework. It includes:
///
/// - [TomException] - Full-featured exception class with logging and server error support
///
/// For the minimal base class without dependencies, see [TomBaseException]
/// from the `tom_basics` package.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Create and throw a tracked exception
/// throw TomException(
///   'user.fetch.not_found',
///   'The requested user could not be found',
///   parameters: {'userId': userId},
///   autoLog: true,
/// );
///
/// // Catch and inspect
/// try {
///   // ... operation that may fail
/// } on TomException catch (e) {
///   print('Error ${e.uuid}: ${e.key}');
///   e.printStackTrace();
/// }
/// ```

classes.documentation:
/// Full-featured exception class with logging, server error tracking, and UUID support.
///
/// [TomException] extends [TomBaseException] with additional features:
///
/// - **Auto Logging**: Optional automatic logging when thrown
/// - **Server Call Error**: Track HTTP-related error details
/// - **TomLoggable**: Integration with the TOM logging system
///
/// For a minimal exception class without these dependencies, use
/// [TomBaseException] from the `tom_basics` package.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Basic exception
/// throw TomException('order.submit.rejected', 'Something went wrong');
///
/// // Exception with parameters for context
/// throw TomException(
///   'signup.validate.invalid_email',
///   'Invalid email format',
///   parameters: {'field': 'email', 'value': userInput},
/// );
///
/// // Exception with auto-logging enabled
/// throw TomException(
///   'storage.connect.unreachable',
///   'Database connection failed',
///   autoLog: true,
///   rootException: originalException,
/// );
/// ```
///
/// See also:
/// - [TomBaseException] for the minimal base class
/// - [TomServerConnectionException] for HTTP-related errors
/// - [TomLoggable] for the logging interface

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a new [TomException] with the given error details.
///
/// The [key] is the error code callers match on, written dotted-lowercase as
/// `<area>.<operation>.<condition>` — the first segment names the module, so
/// keys from unrelated code cannot collide.
/// The [defaultUserMessage] should be human-readable.
///
/// If [autoLog] is true, the exception is logged immediately upon creation.
///
/// When no [stack] is given the trace is captured here, at the construction
/// site, so [printStackTrace] can report where the failure arose.

methods.documentation:
/// Removes this exception's own constructor frames from the captured trace.
///
/// The capture in the initializer above is taken at the last moment the
/// creating code is still on the stack — and therefore also while every
/// constructor in this exception's own chain is. Those frames lead the trace
/// and say nothing: `new TomException` is on every trace in the system, and a
/// subclass adds one more per level. The cost is that a useful `depth` starts
/// at 2 rather than 1, on a hierarchy of ~30 subclasses.
///
/// The run is bounded by **this exception's own leaf constructor**, which is
/// what makes the rule safe. Dropping a leading run of `new …` frames
/// unconditionally would swallow an application constructor that happens to
/// throw — the trace of `MyThing()` raising in its own body opens with
/// `new TomException`, `new MyThing`, and only the first of those is noise.
/// Stopping at `new $runtimeType` keeps `new MyThing`, because for a directly
/// constructed `TomException` the leaf *is* the first frame.
///
/// Both representations are rewritten together. `TomBaseException` derives
/// its `stackTrace` string from `stack` in its constructor, so leaving one
/// trimmed and the other not would have the raw trace and the string an
/// `autoLog` writes disagree about which frame comes first.
///
/// Leaves the trace untouched when the leaf frame is not found, rather than
/// guessing how much to drop: this runs while an exception is being built, so
/// a wrong answer here corrupts the report of some other failure.

/// Logs the stack trace at ERROR level.
///
/// The [depth] parameter limits how many stack frames are included. Any
/// negative value means all frames — -1 is the default and the conventional
/// spelling, not a required sentinel.
///
/// The name is inherited, and so is misleading here: `TomBaseException`
/// genuinely calls [print], this override routes the same report through
/// `tomLog.error` instead. So the destination is whatever the configured
/// log outputs are, not stdout — worth knowing when a caught exception
/// appears to report nothing.
///
/// A `stack` that is not a [StackTrace] is reported as no trace at all — the
/// identifying message alone. See below for why nothing is substituted.

libraries.documentation:
/// Mutual exclusion between everything that might write one file.
///
/// The kernel's file-backed stores mirror an in-memory collection to a JSON
/// file, rewriting it whole on every mutation. [tomWriteFileAtomically] makes
/// each of those writes indivisible, so a reader never sees a half-written
/// file. It does nothing about a *second writer*: two owners of the same path
/// each read it once, each rewrite it whole, and the later write erases
/// everything the earlier one added. Records vanish with no error.
///
/// This library is the guard that makes a mutation a read-modify-write nobody
/// else can interleave with.
///
/// ## Why not `dart:io`'s own file locks
///
/// [RandomAccessFile.lockSync] looks like the obvious answer and is the wrong
/// one: it is **process-scoped**. Two isolates of one process both acquire an
/// "exclusive" lock on the same file and neither is told, because the
/// underlying POSIX locks belong to the process rather than to the opener. A
/// worker isolate and the isolate that spawned it are the first case this
/// guard has to cover, so a primitive that is blind to them cannot be the one.
///
/// Creating a file with `exclusive: true` is a single `O_CREAT | O_EXCL`
/// syscall with no such scoping. Exactly one caller creates it and everyone
/// else gets `EEXIST` — across isolates and across processes alike, on every
/// platform Dart runs on.
///
/// ## The cost of a guard that can be orphaned
///
/// An OS lock dies with the process holding it; a file does not. A writer
/// killed between creating the guard and removing it leaves a file that would
/// block every later owner **forever** — trading silent loss for a permanent
/// hang, which is not a trade worth making.
///
/// So an orphan is broken: a guard whose last-modified time is older than
/// [tomFileLockStaleAfter] is deleted, and the acquirer then re-enters the
/// ordinary exclusive-create race like everyone else. Breaking a guard only
/// removes the obstacle, it never grants the lock — so two waiters that both
/// decide to break cannot both end up holding it; one wins the create and the
/// other keeps waiting. Staleness is re-checked immediately before the delete
/// so the loser of that race cannot then delete the winner's fresh guard.
///
/// The timeout is generous because it is bounded from below by real work: a
/// critical section here is a small read, an encode and one atomic write, so
/// a guard that has stood unchanged for ten seconds is not slow, it is
/// abandoned.
///
/// The library is internal — how a store reaches the disk is not something a
/// caller should reach for — and imports `dart:io`, so nothing web-facing may
/// import it.

functions.documentation:
/// The guard file protecting [path].
///
/// A sibling rather than [path] itself, because the file being protected is
/// replaced by a rename on every write: a guard held *on* it would be a guard
/// on an inode that no longer has the name.

/// Runs [body] with exclusive access to [lockFile] among every isolate and
/// process using this guard, and releases it however [body] ends.
///
/// [onBrokenLock] is called when an abandoned guard had to be broken, since
/// that means some other writer died mid-mutation and its last write may not
/// have landed — worth saying out loud rather than silently recovering from.
/// It is a callback rather than a log call so this library keeps no dependency
/// on the logging module, which is itself one of its callers.
///
/// Throws a [FileSystemException] if the guard cannot be taken within
/// [timeout]. A caller that could not take the lock has not written, and must
/// find that out rather than believe it has persisted.

/// Blocks until this writer holds [lockFile].
///
/// Synchronous on purpose. The stores this guards present a synchronous
/// contract and already block on flushed writes; an asynchronous acquire would
/// let the isolate run other code inside a critical section that exists
/// precisely to keep other code out.

/// Deletes [lockFile] if it has stood unchanged for longer than [staleAfter],
/// returning whether the caller should retry immediately.
///
/// Breaking only removes the obstacle; the caller still has to win the
/// exclusive create afterwards. That is what keeps two simultaneous breakers
/// from both concluding they hold the guard.

/// Releases the guard.
///
/// A failure here is swallowed deliberately: the body has already run, and
/// throwing would report the *work* as failed when only the cleanup was. The
/// staleness recovery in [_acquire] is what covers a guard that outlives its
/// holder, whether through a crash or through this.

variables.documentation:
/// How long a guard may stand unchanged before it is presumed abandoned.
///
/// See the library documentation for why this is generous rather than tight.

/// How long to wait between attempts while another writer holds the guard.
///
/// Short enough that an uncontended handover is not noticeably delayed, long
/// enough that waiting does not become a spin.

libraries.documentation:
/// Formatting utilities for common data transformations.
///
/// This library provides helper functions for formatting data, particularly
/// around time and date handling:
///
/// - [convertMinutesToUtcOffset] - Converts minute offsets to UTC offset strings
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Convert timezone offset to ISO 8601 format
/// print(convertMinutesToUtcOffset(0));    // "Z"
/// print(convertMinutesToUtcOffset(330));  // "+05:30" (IST)
/// print(convertMinutesToUtcOffset(-300)); // "-05:00" (EST)
/// ```
///
/// Offsets are bounded to ±14:00, the range real timezones occupy; anything
/// wider throws [TomFormattingException] rather than producing a string no
/// ISO 8601 parser accepts.

classes.documentation:
/// Thrown when a formatting function is given a value it cannot render.
///
/// The rest of this module deliberately does not throw — [tomGetStackTrace]
/// and friends run while reporting some other failure, so raising there would
/// lose it. That reasoning does not reach here: [convertMinutesToUtcOffset]
/// renders timezone data on the ordinary path, where a value it cannot express
/// is a caller bug worth surfacing rather than a crash report worth saving.

functions.documentation:
/// Converts a UTC offset in minutes to an ISO 8601 formatted string.
///
/// Returns "Z" for zero offset (UTC), otherwise returns a string in the
/// format "+HH:MM" or "-HH:MM".
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [totalMinutes]: The offset from UTC in minutes. Positive values
///   indicate east of UTC, negative values indicate west. Must be within
///   ±840 (±14:00) inclusive — the range real timezones occupy.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// An ISO 8601 formatted UTC offset string.
///
/// ## Throws
///
/// [TomFormattingException] (`formatting.utc_offset.out_of_range`) when
/// [totalMinutes] lies outside ±840. The hour field is two digits wide, so a
/// larger offset could only be rendered as something like `+100:00` — a string
/// no ISO 8601 parser accepts. Failing is the honest outcome: the value did
/// not come from a timezone, and there is no correct way to write it.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(0);     // "Z" (UTC)
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(60);    // "+01:00" (CET)
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(330);   // "+05:30" (IST)
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(-480);  // "-08:00" (PST)
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(840);   // "+14:00" (Line Islands, the maximum)
/// convertMinutesToUtcOffset(6000);  // throws TomFormattingException
/// ```

variables.documentation:
/// The widest UTC offset, in minutes, that [convertMinutesToUtcOffset] can
/// render — ±14:00.
///
/// Measured rather than assumed. Scanning every location's transition table in
/// the packaged IANA data yields reachable offsets from -720 (Pacific/Kanton)
/// to +840 (Asia/Anadyr) and nothing outside, so this bound never rejects a
/// value the timezone API can produce. It is *inclusive* precisely because
/// Asia/Anadyr sits exactly on it; an exclusive bound would break a real zone.
///
/// The raw `zones` tables do carry a few wider historical LMT records — Asia/
/// Manila at -956, America/Metlakatla at +913 — but no transition selects
/// them, so they are unreachable and do not widen the bound.

libraries.documentation:
/// Crash-safe whole-file writes for the kernel's file-backed stores.
///
/// Two stores in this package mirror an in-memory collection to a JSON file on
/// every mutation — `TomFileJobStore` for the scheduler's durable queue and
/// `TomFileLogReplayStore` for buffered log records. Both rewrite the file
/// whole, and both are read back by a loader that can do nothing with a
/// truncated array but discard it. A plain overwrite therefore turns any
/// interruption mid-write — a kill, a power loss, a full disk — into *silent
/// total loss* of exactly the data the store existed to protect.
///
/// This library holds the one write routine both of them use.
///
/// The library is internal: it is not exported from `tom_core_kernel.dart`,
/// because "how a store reaches the disk" is not something a caller should be
/// reaching for. It imports `dart:io` and is safe to keep here only because
/// nothing web-facing imports it.

functions.documentation:
/// The sibling path [tomWriteFileAtomically] stages the new content at before
/// renaming it over [path].
///
/// The writer's identity is part of the name on purpose. A staging name shared
/// between writers lets one writer's rename publish another's half-written
/// file — reintroducing, between writers, the very truncation this library
/// exists to prevent. One name per writer per target also means staging files
/// cannot accumulate: a writer reuses its own.

/// Writes [contents] to [file] so a reader sees either the complete previous
/// content or the complete new content, never a blend of the two.
///
/// The bytes go to the sibling staging file named by [tomAtomicWriteTempPath],
/// flushed so they are on the device rather than in the OS write-back cache,
/// and that file is then renamed over [file]. Rename within a directory is
/// atomic, which is what buys the guarantee: the target is only ever replaced
/// by a file that was already written in full.
///
/// Missing parent directories are created.
///
/// Throws whatever the underlying filesystem operation throws — a caller that
/// cannot persist must find out. If the staging write fails part-way, the
/// partial file is removed before the error propagates, so a full disk does not
/// leave debris that makes the next attempt harder.

variables.documentation:
/// Identifies *this isolate* among everything that might write the same file.
///
/// A process id is not enough. Every isolate in a process reports the same
/// [pid], so naming a per-writer file after the pid alone gives two isolates
/// one name — and for the staging file below that means one isolate's rename
/// publishes, or destroys, the file the other is still writing.
///
/// The random half is what separates isolates, and it must be *unpredictably*
/// random rather than merely varied: this is computed once per isolate, and
/// isolates spawned in the same instant would draw the same seed from a
/// time-based generator and collide exactly when concurrency is highest.

libraries.documentation:
/// Core utility functions for zone management and stack trace handling.
///
/// This library provides essential utility functions used throughout the
/// tom_core framework:
///
/// - **Zone Utilities**: Access typed values from the current Dart zone
/// - **Checked Execution**: Run code with error transformation
/// - **Stack Trace Processing**: Format and analyze stack traces
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Access typed values from current zone
/// final config = getFromCurrentZone<AppConfig>();
///
/// // Run with error transformation
/// final result = await runChecked(
///   () => fetchData(),
///   (error, stack) => TomException('catalog.fetch.failed', 'Fetch failed',
///       rootException: error, stack: stack),
/// );
///
/// // Get formatted stack trace
/// print(tomGetStackTrace(StackTrace.current, 5));
/// ```

functions.documentation:
/// Retrieves a typed value from the current Dart [Zone].
///
/// Zone values are used for passing context through async operations without
/// explicitly threading parameters through function calls.
///
/// ## The key is the exact `Type`, not an assignable one
///
/// The lookup is `Zone.current[T]` — a map lookup whose key is the `Type`
/// object [T] itself, matched by `==`. [Zone] exposes no way to enumerate its
/// keys, so **no subtype scan is possible** and none is performed.
/// Assignability plays no part in *finding* the value:
///
/// ```dart
/// runZoned(
///   () {
///     getFromCurrentZone<Derived>();  // the instance
///     getFromCurrentZone<Base>();     // null — a different key
///     getFromCurrentZone<Object>();   // null — `Object` is not a wildcard
///   },
///   zoneValues: {Derived: Derived()},
/// );
/// ```
///
/// It fails in the other direction too: a `Derived` instance filed under the
/// `Base` key is found by `getFromCurrentZone<Base>()` and *not* by
/// `getFromCurrentZone<Derived>()`. `implements` creates no relationship
/// either, and type arguments are part of the key — `List<int>`, `List` and
/// `List<num>` are three distinct keys, as are `T` and `T?`.
///
/// **So the producer and every consumer must name the same type.** Choose the
/// key once — conventionally the declared type of the field the value is read
/// into — and use it on both sides. A mismatch is not a compile error and
/// raises nothing; it is a silent `null`, which is also what "nothing stored
/// here" looks like.
///
/// ## Two independent reasons for `null`
///
/// Returns `null` when nothing is stored under the key [T], **and** when what
/// is stored under that key is not a [T]. The key lookup is the first and
/// stricter filter; the `is T` guard that follows can only narrow what the key
/// already found. [getObjectFromCurrentZone] tells the two apart — it applies
/// the same key lookup without the guard.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Store a value in a zone
/// runZoned(
///   () {
///     final config = getFromCurrentZone<AppConfig>();
///     print(config?.environment);
///   },
///   zoneValues: {AppConfig: AppConfig(environment: 'production')},
/// );
/// ```
///
/// ## Type Parameter
///
/// - [T]: The type of value to retrieve. Must be the *same* type that was used
///   as the key when the value was stored — not merely a type the stored value
///   happens to satisfy.

/// Retrieves an untyped value from the current Dart [Zone] by its [Type].
///
/// Unlike [getFromCurrentZone], this returns the raw [Object?] without
/// type checking. Useful when the type is only known at runtime, and when the
/// caller needs to distinguish "nothing stored under this key" from "something
/// stored, but not of the expected type" — [getFromCurrentZone] answers `null`
/// to both.
///
/// Dropping the guard does not relax the key lookup: [t] is matched exactly,
/// with the same consequences described on [getFromCurrentZone]. A value stored
/// under a subtype of [t] is not found here either.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [t]: The [Type] to use as the zone value key. Must be the same type the
///   producer used, not merely a supertype of the stored value.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// The zone value stored under [t], or `null` if that key is absent.

/// Runs an async function with error transformation.
///
/// Executes [body] and catches any errors, transforming them using [onError]
/// before rethrowing. This is useful for wrapping low-level errors in
/// domain-specific exceptions.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final data = await runChecked(
///   () => httpClient.get(url),
///   (error, stacktrace) => TomException(
///     'catalog.fetch.unreachable',
///     'Request failed',
///     rootException: error,
///     stack: stacktrace,
///   ),
/// );
/// ```
///
/// [onError] returns `Object`, so any type will do; returning a [TomException]
/// is what carries its `error` and `stacktrace` arguments forward rather than
/// discarding them.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [body]: The async function to execute.
/// - [onError]: Transform function called when [body] throws.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// The result of [body] if successful.
///
/// ## Throws
///
/// The transformed error from [onError] if [body] throws.

/// Synchronous version of [runChecked].
///
/// Executes [body] synchronously and catches any errors, transforming them
/// using [onError] before rethrowing.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final config = runCheckedSync(
///   () => parseConfig(jsonString),
///   (error, stacktrace) => TomException(
///     'config.parse.invalid_format',
///     'Invalid config format',
///     rootException: error,
///     stack: stacktrace,
///   ),
/// );
/// ```

/// Logs a formatted stack trace at ERROR level.
///
/// Formats the trace with [tomGetStackTrace], prefixes it with [message] when
/// one is given, and passes the result to `tomLog.error`. Despite the name it
/// does **not** call [print]: the text goes wherever the configured log
/// outputs point, so under a recording output in a test it never reaches
/// stdout, and under a filtered one it is dropped entirely.
///
/// Nothing is logged when there would be nothing to say — no message and no
/// frames. That combination is reachable three ways: `depth: 0`, which is a
/// meaningful request for no frames; [StackTrace.empty]; and a trace whose
/// every frame was filtered out as noise. In each case an unconditional call
/// emitted an ERROR record with an empty body, which is worse than silence: it
/// asserts that something failed, describes nothing, and does so at the level
/// operators alert on.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [s]: The stack trace, or `null` to use [StackTrace.current].
/// - [message]: Optional prefix message for the log entry.
/// - [depth]: Maximum number of frames to include. Any negative value means
///   all frames — -1 is the conventional spelling, not a required sentinel.

/// Core implementation for processing stack trace frames.
///
/// Folds and filters stack frames to remove noise from core libraries,
/// shelf, TomLogger internals and this library's own formatting functions,
/// then flattens every segment of the async chain into one list.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [s]: The stack trace to process. Defaults to [StackTrace.current].
/// - [depth]: Maximum frames to return. Any negative value means all frames —
///   -1 is the conventional spelling, not a required sentinel.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// A list of formatted stack frame strings, newest first. Empty when the trace
/// carries no frames — see [StackTrace.empty] below.

/// Formats a stack trace as a single string.
///
/// Processes the stack trace, filtering out framework noise, and returns
/// it as a newline-separated string suitable for logging.
///
/// An asynchronous trace is reported **whole**: the runtime splits it into one
/// segment per `await` boundary, and every segment is included, newest first.
/// So the frames run from the throw site back through each awaiting caller to
/// the one that started the work — which is where an async failure's cause
/// usually is.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [s]: The stack trace, or `null` to use [StackTrace.current].
/// - [depth]: Maximum number of frames to include. Any negative value means
///   all frames — -1 is the conventional spelling, not a required sentinel.
///   The limit applies across the whole chain, counting from the throw site.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// A formatted stack trace string with one frame per line. An empty string
/// when the trace has no frames — [StackTrace.empty] is what Dart supplies for
/// an error created without one, so this is an ordinary outcome rather than an
/// error.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// try {
///   riskyOperation();
/// } catch (e, stack) {
///   log.error('Error: $e\n${tomGetStackTrace(stack, 10)}');
/// }
/// ```

/// Formats a stack trace as a list of strings.
///
/// Same as [tomGetStackTrace] but returns individual frames as list elements.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [s]: The stack trace, or `null` to use [StackTrace.current].
/// - [depth]: Maximum number of frames to include. Any negative value means
///   all frames — -1 is the conventional spelling, not a required sentinel.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// A list of formatted stack frame strings, empty when the trace has no
/// frames.

/// Formats a stack trace as both a string and list.
///
/// Convenience function that returns both representations in a single call.
/// Processes the trace exactly as [tomGetStackTrace] does, async chain
/// included.
///
/// ## Parameters
///
/// - [s]: The stack trace, or `null` to use [StackTrace.current].
/// - [depth]: Maximum number of frames to include. Any negative value means
///   all frames — -1 is the conventional spelling, not a required sentinel.
///
/// ## Returns
///
/// A tuple of (formatted string, list of frame strings).
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final (stackString, stackList) = tomGetStackTraceAsTuple();
/// print('Full trace: $stackString');
/// print('First frame: ${stackList.first}');
/// ```

variables.documentation:
/// The stack-trace formatting functions of this library.
///
/// These appear on a trace only when it was captured *inside* the formatter —
/// that is, whenever the caller passed no stack and [StackTrace.current] was
/// used as the fallback. They record the act of formatting, never the failure,
/// so they are noise in every trace they appear in.
///
/// Matching is on the member name rather than the library, because the library
/// URI of this file differs between `package:` and `file:` resolution and a
/// consumer's trace may carry either form.

classes.documentation:
/// Raised when a scope cannot be built or used as one.
///
/// Keys are `scope_ids.<operation>.<condition>`:
/// `scope_ids.init_scope.empty_id`, `scope_ids.init_scope.separator_in_id`,
/// and `scope_ids.use_scope.no_identity`.

/// A hierarchical, zone-propagated identifier used to namespace resource
/// lookups (e.g. `app.checkout.orderForm`).
///
/// Scopes form a parent chain rooted at [defaultScope]. The active scope is
/// carried on the current [Zone], so [current] resolves to whichever scope was
/// installed by the nearest enclosing [withScope] / [runInScope] call.

methods.documentation:
/// Runs [body] in a new child scope named [scope], parented to [current].
///
/// The scope rides on a child [Zone], which bounds *reachability*, not
/// lifetime. The caller's own continuation after this call never sees the
/// scope — but anything [body] schedules (a `Future`, `Timer` or stream
/// subscription) inherits the zone and observes the scope whenever it later
/// runs. A fire-and-forget `Future` started here therefore keeps this scope
/// for its entire life, however long that outlasts [body].
///
/// Zone inheritance covers callbacks the async machinery schedules; it does
/// not cover a closure that is merely *stored* here and invoked directly by
/// someone else later, which runs in the invoker's scope. Wrap those with
/// [ScopeCapture].

/// Runs [body] inside this scope's zone.
///
/// If this scope is already the current zone scope (identical reference),
/// [body] is executed directly without creating a new zone.
/// This prevents duplicate scope nesting.

/// Builds the scope path for any TomScope instance.
/// Returns a dot-separated string (e.g., "app.checkout.orderForm").
///
/// **The parent chain must be acyclic.** The walk is iterative and
/// unguarded, so a loop does not raise `StackOverflowError` — it spins
/// silently, appending segments until the heap is exhausted and the isolate
/// dies. In a server that is the whole process.
///
/// No guard is needed for scopes this module builds, because none of them can
/// form a loop: [withScope] and the [TomScopeBase] constructor take a parent
/// that already exists, and [TomScopeMixin.initScope] refuses one that is not
/// yet initialised and then writes the link once. Every parent is therefore
/// strictly older than its child, and a chain that only ever moves backwards
/// in time cannot return to where it started.
///
/// The requirement binds anything that implements [TomScope] by hand: such a
/// type supplies its own [parent], outside that argument and outside any
/// check this module can make.

fields.documentation:
/// The root scope used when no scope is installed on the current zone.
///
/// Immutable by design: it is the root of every scope path, so reassigning it
/// at runtime would change resource resolution for the whole process. Use
/// [withScope] or [runInScope] instead — they install a scope on a child
/// zone, so the change reaches that body and the callbacks it schedules,
/// never the caller or the rest of the process.

classes.documentation:
/// Captures a [TomScope] at construction time and wraps callback functions
/// so they execute within that scope when called later.
///
/// Flutter calls builder callbacks (e.g., `itemBuilder`, `builder`) outside
/// the original zone. This wrapper ensures Tom widgets created inside those
/// callbacks still get the correct scope for resource resolution.
///
/// Usage in Tom widget constructors:
/// ```dart
/// class TomFoo extends TomNodeBase {
///   final WidgetBuilder _builder;
///   TomFoo({required WidgetBuilder builder, super.scope})
///       : _builder = ScopeCapture(scope: scope).wrap1(builder);
/// }
/// ```

methods.documentation:
/// Wraps a callback with one positional and named parameters.
/// Use for Flutter's `InputCounterWidgetBuilder` and similar typedefs
/// that use named parameters.

mixins.documentation:
/// Reusable [TomScope] implementation backing [TomScopeBase].
///
/// Mix into a class and call [initScope] exactly once to set the scope's
/// identity — usually from the constructor. The backing fields are write-once,
/// and [initScope] is the only thing that writes them.

methods.documentation:
/// Initialises this scope's identity.
///
/// The single write-once entry point: call it exactly once, from the
/// constructor or — for a scope whose parent is only known later — on the
/// constructed instance. [TomScopeBase] and [TomScope.withScope] both route
/// here, so the checks below hold for every scope the module builds.
///
/// [scopeId] must be non-empty and must not contain `.`, because ids are
/// joined with `.` to form the scope path. Both are refused with a
/// [TomScopeException]: an id that carries the separator forges a segment
/// boundary, so the scope renders as a path belonging to a different, deeper
/// chain — and the path is what resource and authorization lookups are keyed
/// on. Ids written as literals in source can never trip this; ids derived
/// from data are exactly what the check is for.
///
/// [parentScope] must already have an identity of its own. Checking it here
/// rather than when the path is built is what keeps the check O(1): an
/// initialised scope then has a fully initialised chain by induction, since
/// the identity is write-once and a parent cannot lose one later.
///
/// The same rule is also what keeps the chain **acyclic**, which
/// [TomScope.buildScopePath] relies on and does not verify. Requiring the
/// parent to be initialised already makes it strictly older than its child,
/// and writing the link once means it stays that way — so following parents
/// always moves backwards in time and can never arrive back at where it
/// started. Closing a loop would need this call to accept a scope that is not
/// yet initialised, or to run a second time on a scope that already is;
/// neither is allowed.

fields.documentation:
/// Whether [initScope] has run.
///
/// Tracked separately because Dart offers no way to ask a `late` field
/// whether it has been written — reading it to find out throws, which is the
/// very failure this flag exists to pre-empt.

functions.documentation:
/// Shared scope-installation policy for [TomScope.runInScope] and
/// [TomScopeMixin.runInScope].
///
/// Every install goes through here, which is what makes the identity check
/// below a property of the module rather than of one call site.
///
/// If [scope] is already the current zone scope (identical reference), [body]
/// runs directly without creating a new zone — this prevents duplicate scope
/// nesting. Otherwise [body] runs in a child zone carrying [scope].

/// Refuses a [TomScopeMixin] instance whose [TomScopeMixin.initScope] has never
/// run, naming the class and the missing call.
///
/// [attemptedAction] opens the message, so the reader learns what was refused
/// before why. Anything that is not an uninitialised mixin passes: a
/// hand-rolled `implements TomScope` has no initialisation window this module
/// knows about.
///
/// Without this, a half-built instance installs silently and the failure
/// surfaces at whichever later call first asks for a path — a resource lookup
/// or an authorization check, on a stack that mentions neither scopes nor the
/// constructor that skipped [TomScopeMixin.initScope].

/// Refuses a scope id that cannot survive being joined into a path.
///
/// The path is this module's entire output — a prefix for resource and
/// authorization lookups — and it is built by joining ids with
/// [_scopePathSeparator]. An id containing the separator therefore forges a
/// segment boundary: `'a.b'` under no parent renders exactly as the unrelated
/// chain `'a'` → `'b'`, so a scope named from data (a tenant, a row key, a
/// user-supplied label) can name another scope's resources. An empty id is the
/// same defect seen from the other end — it renders as a bare `''` at the root,
/// which consumers are told to read as "no scope at all", or as a doubled
/// separator further down.
///
/// Checked once per scope, at the only place an id is ever accepted, rather
/// than on every path build.

libraries.documentation:
/// Server connection and HTTP client module.
///
/// This library provides a comprehensive HTTP client infrastructure for making
/// server requests. It includes:
///
/// - [TomServerCall] - Low-level HTTP request handling with abort support
/// - [TomServerChannel] - HTTP client management with connection pooling
/// - [TomServerEndpoint] - Type-safe endpoint execution with serialization
/// - [TomHttpClientFactory] - Pluggable HTTP client creation
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Create a channel to a server
/// final channel = TomServerChannel('api.example.com', 443);
///
/// // Create and execute a server call
/// final call = channel.prepareCall(TomServerCallSpecs(
///   'POST',
///   Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/users'),
/// ));
///
/// call.send(TomServerCallLoad(
///   stringBody: '{"name": "John"}',
///   contentType: 'application/json',
/// ));
///
/// final response = await call.readAsString(utf8);
/// ```

classes.documentation:
/// Exception thrown when a server connection error occurs.
///
/// This includes errors such as:
/// - Invalid request state (e.g., calling send twice)
/// - Reading before sending
/// - Network errors

/// Exception thrown when a request is aborted.
///
/// This can occur when [TomServerCall.abort] is called during a pending request.

/// Exception thrown when a request outlives its [TomServerCallSpecs.timeout].
///
/// Deliberately *not* a subtype of [TomRequestAbortedException], even though a
/// timeout aborts the request on its way out: the two mean opposite things to
/// the caller. An abort is this application's own decision and repeating it
/// would be pointless; a timeout is the server failing to answer in time, which
/// is the textbook case for a retry. A caller that wants to treat them alike can
/// catch [TomException]; one that wants to retry only timeouts could not
/// separate them again if this were a subclass.

/// Specification for an HTTP server call.
///
/// Contains all the configuration needed to make an HTTP request,
/// including method, URL, headers, and connection settings.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final specs = TomServerCallSpecs(
///   'POST',
///   Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/users'),
///   headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
///   followRedirects: true,
/// );
/// ```

fields.documentation:
/// HTTP headers to include in the request, empty when the caller named none.
///
/// Non-null and mutable, on the same terms as [TomServerCallError.headers]:
/// the constructor copies what it is given, so the map is this spec's own and
/// writing to it always works. A nullable field with a `const {}` default
/// would be the worst of both: never actually null, so every `!= null` test
/// against it is true, and unmodifiable, so every write to it throws.

/// How long [TomServerCall.read] may wait before giving up, or `null` for no
/// deadline.
///
/// The budget covers the whole exchange — waiting for the response and
/// draining its body — rather than restarting per stage, because a server
/// that sends headers promptly and then stalls mid-body hangs the caller just
/// as effectively as one that never answers.
///
/// `null` by default: a deadline that suits a JSON call would truncate a long
/// upload, and this type cannot tell which it is being used for. The caller
/// knows, so the caller states it.

classes.documentation:
/// Payload data for an HTTP request.
///
/// Supports three types of body content:
/// - [stringBody] - Text content (JSON, XML, etc.)
/// - [byteBody] - Binary content (files, images, etc.)
/// - [bodyFields] - Form fields for URL-encoded requests
///
/// Only one type of body can be set at a time, and a load never changes after
/// it is built: every field is final, so the constructor's check is a promise
/// about the whole object rather than about one moment in its life. A load that
/// exists describes exactly one request body — no consumer has to re-check it.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // JSON body
/// final jsonLoad = TomServerCallLoad(
///   stringBody: '{"name": "John"}',
///   contentType: 'application/json',
///   encoding: utf8,
/// );
///
/// // Form data
/// final formLoad = TomServerCallLoad(
///   bodyFields: {'username': 'john', 'password': 'secret'},
/// );
/// ```

constructors.documentation:
/// Creates a new request payload.
///
/// Only one of [stringBody], [byteBody], or [bodyFields] may be set; supplying
/// more than one throws [TomServerConnectionException]. Because the fields are
/// final, passing that check is the only way a load can come into existence.
///
/// A [bodyFields] body must also be labelled as a form, but that check is not
/// here: the label can equally come from [TomServerCallSpecs.headers], which
/// a load never sees. It is made once, against the request's effective
/// content type, in [TomServerCall.applyLoadValues].

fields.documentation:
/// The `Content-Type` header value, or `null` to let the body choose one.
///
/// Applied to the request ahead of the body, so `package:http` labels the
/// bytes with what the caller named. Left `null`, the body picks the default
/// its own shape implies: `text/plain` for [stringBody] and
/// `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` for [bodyFields], both carrying the
/// [encoding]'s charset. [byteBody] implies nothing and stays unlabelled.
///
/// There is deliberately no `contentLength` beside this. A `Content-Type` is
/// the caller's to state — the bytes do not say what they mean — whereas a
/// `Content-Length` is a fact about the body that the request measures for
/// itself, and that this module's non-streaming request refuses to be told.
/// Streaming uploads, where the length genuinely is unknown until it is
/// declared, go through [TomHttpFileTransferClient] rather than through a
/// load.

/// Form field body (mutually exclusive with [stringBody] and [byteBody]).
///
/// A copy of what the constructor was given, so later edits to the caller's
/// map cannot add a body to a load that was built without one.

classes.documentation:
/// Represents an error or response status from a server call.
///
/// Contains detailed information about the server response including:
/// - HTTP status code
/// - Response body
/// - Headers
/// - Error details from Tom server errors
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final (response, error) = await endpoint.send(request);
/// if (error?.hasError ?? false) {
///   print('Error: ${error!.statusCode} - ${error.reasonPhrase}');
/// }
/// ```

methods.documentation:
/// Whether [statusCode] is in the HTTP 2xx success range.
///
/// The one definition of "the server accepted this" in the module. Both
/// [hasError] and the response handling in [TomServerEndpoint.send] read it,
/// because when each spelled the test for itself they disagreed: `send`
/// treated anything other than exactly 200 as a failure, so a `201 Created`
/// was routed away from the deserializer while `hasError` went on reporting
/// no error.

/// Whether a response with [statusCode] is defined to carry no body.
///
/// `204 No Content` and `205 Reset Content` have no payload by definition
/// (RFC 9110 §15.3.5–6), so for those an empty body is the expected shape and
/// there is nothing to deserialize — as opposed to a status that promised a
/// body and sent none, which stays a failure to parse.

fields.documentation:
/// Internal error code when no response was received.
///
/// Raised by [TomServerEndpoint.send] when the request never produced a
/// response — a refused connection, an expired deadline, an aborted request.
/// Such an error carries no status (`statusCode` is 0) and no body; the
/// underlying failure is in [exception].

/// The deserialized body, whichever type it was read as.
///
/// On a success this is the same object [TomServerEndpoint.send] returns as
/// its result. On a failure whose status has an entry in the endpoint's
/// `statusCodeResponseTypes` it is an instance of *that* type; on a failure
/// with no entry it stays null.

/// Response headers from the server, empty when there were none.
///
/// Always a mutable map belonging to this error. The constructor copies what
/// it is given rather than keeping the caller's map, for two reasons: a
/// response that carried no headers supplies a `const {}` that would reject
/// every write, and [TomServerEndpoint.send] hands over the live response
/// header map, which annotating an error would otherwise rewrite in place.

classes.documentation:
/// Handles a single HTTP request/response cycle.
///
/// Provides methods to send a request, read the response, and abort
/// pending requests. Each instance can only be used once - call [reset]
/// to reuse.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final client = Client();
/// final specs = TomServerCallSpecs('GET', Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/data'));
/// final call = TomServerCall(client, specs);
///
/// call.send(TomServerCallLoad());
/// final bytes = await call.read();
/// final response = utf8.decode(bytes);
/// ```

methods.documentation:
/// Applies payload values to the request.
///
/// Writes whichever body [load] carries. It carries at most one — the type
/// checks that when it is built and cannot be changed afterwards — so nothing
/// is re-checked here. Were two ever to arrive, the fields would be applied in
/// order and `package:http` would reject the second against the content type
/// the first had already set, leaving the request half-written.
///
/// The order of the two lines that precede the body is load-bearing, because
/// `package:http` reads both back when the body is assigned. The encoding is
/// the charset the body is encoded with, and the content type is the header
/// that charset is then written into; a type applied *after* the body would
/// be a type the body never saw, replacing the labelling `package:http` had
/// just derived and dropping the charset with it.

/// Refuses a form body that [contentType] labels as something else.
///
/// `package:http` encodes form fields as `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`
/// and rejects any other declared type — with a bare `StateError` that names
/// neither the call nor the setting that disagreed, raised partway through
/// writing the request.
///
/// The check belongs here rather than on [TomServerCallLoad] because the
/// label has two sources: the load's own `contentType` and the spec's
/// `headers`. Only the request has seen both, so a constructor check would
/// pass every load whose spec carried the offending header — a guard that
/// reads as complete and is not.
///
/// A type that merely carries parameters — `...urlencoded; charset=utf-8` —
/// is the same type, so the media types are compared rather than the strings.
/// An unparseable value is left alone: `package:http` reports a malformed
/// content type in its own terms, and guessing here would name the wrong
/// fault.

/// Resets the call for reuse with new body content.
///
/// Clears the response and creates a new abortable request.

/// Sends the request with the given payload.
///
/// Can only be called once per call instance (use [reset] to retry).
/// Returns this instance for method chaining.
///
/// The rejection of an already-sent call happens before anything is applied,
/// so a refused `send` leaves the request describing what was actually sent. A
/// rejection that still mutated would leave the object describing a request
/// that never happened, which a caller inspecting it after the throw, or a
/// later [reset], would read back as real.
///
/// [load] needs no check here: it is immutable and validated its single body
/// on construction, so the value that arrives is the value that was built.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] if called more than once.

/// Refuses a read of [what] before [send], under the one key
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send`.
///
/// The key lives here rather than at each reader, for the same reason it does
/// in [_ensureNotYetRead]: a key's last segment names the *condition* (see
/// [TomException]), and "nothing has been sent" is one condition however many
/// members can meet it. Supplied by the caller it became seven keys for that
/// one condition — six accessors each naming itself, plus [read] — so code
/// handling "this call has not been sent" had to match seven, and an eighth
/// reader would have brought an eighth.
///
/// [what] names in prose the value the caller was after ("the response
/// headers"). It is the detail those seven keys were carrying, and it reaches
/// the message and the `read` parameter instead, where a detail belongs and
/// costs the contract nothing. **A new reader passes only [what]** — there is
/// no key to choose, so the convention holds without anyone deciding again.

/// Awaits the in-flight response, refusing a read of [what] before [send]
/// exactly as [_requireSent] does.
///
/// Split from [_requireSent] because [read] needs the guard *without* the
/// await: it defers awaiting the response into [_awaitTransfer] so that its
/// timeout covers the headers as well as the body.

/// Guards against reading the response more than once, throwing
/// [TomServerConnectionException] when the body has already been consumed.
///
/// The key lives here rather than at each reader: there is one "already
/// consumed" condition, so it has to raise one key. Letting each caller pass
/// its own turned a single condition into a contract that could drift — and
/// it did, one reader having spelled the key `read_only_once`.

/// Reads the response body as bytes.
///
/// Can only be called once after [send] — the body is a stream, and draining
/// it leaves nothing for a second reader. Every reader reaches the body
/// through here, so all of them refuse a second read under the one key
/// `server.send.illegal_state_only_read_once`.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send,
/// or `server.send.illegal_state_only_read_once` if called again.
/// Throws [TomRequestAbortedException] if the request was aborted.

/// Awaits the response and drains its body, translating an abort raised by
/// either stage into [TomRequestAbortedException].
///
/// Split out of [read] so that a single deadline can be placed around both
/// stages at once, and so that an abort arriving *before* the headers do is
/// reported the same way as one arriving mid-body.

/// Reads the response body as a string with the given encoding.
///
/// Can only be called once after [send], and refuses a second read exactly as
/// [read] does — it *is* [read], with a decode on the end, so it carries no
/// guard of its own.

/// Gets the response headers after sending.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Gets the HTTP status code after sending.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Gets the response content length after sending.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Checks if the response is a redirect after sending.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Gets the reason phrase after sending (e.g., "OK", "Not Found").
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Gets the original request from the response after sending.
///
/// Throws [TomServerConnectionException] under
/// `server.send.illegal_state_read_must_be_after_send` if called before send.

/// Aborts the pending request.
///
/// Causes [read] to throw [TomRequestAbortedException].

