methods.documentation:
/// Builds the URL that addresses this endpoint on the server rooted at
/// [baseUri], substituting [parameters] into the declaration.
///
/// This is the **client-side inverse** of the server pipeline's URL-parameter
/// binding. The server folds a request's path captures and its named query
/// parameters *into* a typed request DTO; this builds the URL those values
/// travelled on, from the same [uri] template and the same
/// [urlParameterMapping]. Keeping both directions derived from one declaration
/// is the whole point: a client that spelled the path itself would agree with
/// the route only by inspection, and would keep agreeing only by luck.
///
/// [parameters] is keyed by **request-DTO field name** — the *values* of
/// [urlParameterMapping] — because a client holds a DTO, not a URL. The two
/// kinds of parameter then bind under the rules the mapping documents:
///
/// * **Path captures** (`{name}` in [uri], one per path segment) take
///   `parameters[urlParameterMapping[name] ?? name]`, so an unmapped capture
///   binds by its own name. A capture with no value is an error, not an empty
///   segment: the URL would address a different route.
/// * **Query parameters** are the mapping entries whose field the path did
///   *not* already carry. Each is appended when [parameters] holds a
///   non-`null` value for its field, and omitted otherwise — an optional query
///   parameter is ordinary, whereas a missing path capture is a broken
///   address. Skipping by field rather than by URL name is what keeps
///   capture-wins true when two keys map to one field: the value goes in the
///   path once instead of the path and the query both.
///
/// Values are converted with `toString()` and escaped per path segment, so a
/// key containing `/` or `?` addresses one segment rather than smuggling a
/// path or a query string past the route.
///
/// ```dart
/// final read = tomUserPreferencesApi[tomUserPreferencesReadEndpointId];
/// read.resolveUri(
///   Uri.parse('https://api.example.com'),
///   parameters: {'key': TomUserPreferenceKeys.theme},
/// ); // https://api.example.com/preferences/tom.theme
/// ```
///
/// Throws [TomEndpointException] when the endpoint has no parent API, or when
/// a path capture has no answering parameter.

fields.documentation:
/// How long a client call to this endpoint may take before it gives up, or
/// `null` to wait indefinitely.
///
/// Declared per endpoint rather than per API because the right deadline is a
/// property of the work behind the URL, not of the service as a whole: a
/// report that legitimately takes a minute sits in the same API as a lookup
/// that should answer in under a second, and one shared value would have to
/// be the larger of the two — which is the same as having none.

/// Binds URL parameters — path captures *and* query-string parameters — to
/// typed request-DTO fields.
///
/// Keys are the parameter names as they appear on the URL: a route-capture
/// name from [uri] (e.g. `id` in `/items/{id}`), or a query-string parameter
/// name (e.g. `error_description` in `?error_description=…`). Values are the
/// request-DTO field names the value is injected under before the DTO is
/// instantiated. When the URL name and the field name are identical, the entry
/// is `{'id': 'id'}`; a differently named field is `{'id': 'itemId'}`, which is
/// also how a provider's snake_cased spelling is translated into a Dart name.
///
/// The two kinds bind under different rules, deliberately:
///
/// * **Path captures** not listed are still injected under their own capture
///   name (identity), so an empty map means every path parameter binds by its
///   own name. A capture is part of the route, and the route is part of this
///   endpoint's own declaration.
/// * **Query parameters** bind *only* when named here — there is no identity
///   fallback. A query string is whatever a caller chose to append, so an
///   identity fallback would let any caller write any field of the request DTO.
///   Naming them makes query binding opt-in per endpoint and per parameter.
///
/// A path capture wins over a query parameter mapped to the same field — the
/// route is the stronger statement. The rule is about the **field**, not the
/// URL name that spells it: when two keys map to one field and the first is a
/// capture, the second is not also emitted as a query parameter. The server
/// gets this structurally (it folds query values into the request DTO first
/// and lets the path overwrite them under the same field name);
/// [TomApiEndpoint.resolveUri] applies the same rule when building the URL.
///
/// A path capture wins over a **body** field of the same name too, by the
/// same mechanism — the server folds the JSON body in first and lets the URL
/// overwrite it. Do not rely on that. Precedence is the answer to a question
/// a contract should not be asking: a request that states its subject twice
/// can state it two different ways, and a caller that reads the body as
/// authoritative is wrong without ever being told. Declare a capture for a
/// field the request DTO *does not otherwise carry* — or, when the DTO must
/// carry it, declare no capture and let the body be the single statement.
/// The `tomUserPreferencesApi` write endpoint is the worked example: it takes
/// a key, its request has a `key` field, so its URI is the bare collection.

/// The access policy this endpoint is served under, **declared here on the
/// contract** (G-API-3). The server pipeline extracts it when the endpoint's
/// implementation handler carries no local `TomAccessControl` annotation of
/// its own; a handler annotation, when present, overrides this declaration.
///
/// `null` means the endpoint declares no contract-side policy, so the server
/// falls back to its global protection default (or a handler annotation). Any
/// `TomAccessControl` (`TomPublicAccess`, `TomAuthenticatedAccess`,
/// `TomRoleAccess`, …) is a valid value; the constants are usually `const`, so
/// one shared policy can be referenced by many endpoints without re-stating it.

libraries.documentation:
/// Streaming file transfer — the client half of the framework's file storage.
///
/// The server side (`tom_core_server`'s `file_storage` module) stores a file as
/// a stream and reads it back as a stream, so a large attachment never has to
/// exist in memory at either end. This library is the matching client contract:
/// a [TomFileSource] to send, a [TomFileMetadata] to describe what was stored,
/// and a [TomFileTransferClient] that moves bytes between them with progress
/// reporting and cancellation.
///
/// ## Why this is not the existing [TomServerCall]
///
/// `TomServerCall` is a request/response API: a body is a `String`, a
/// `Uint8List` or form fields, and `read()` accumulates the whole response into
/// a `BytesBuilder`. That is the right shape for JSON endpoints and the wrong
/// shape for a 400 MB video. This library keeps the same connection machinery
/// ([TomServerChannel], the bearer token, the client factory) but never
/// materialises the payload.
///
/// ## Slots, not keys
///
/// A transfer addresses a **slot** — an application-owned path such as
/// `invoices/4711/scan` — and never a storage key. Storage keys are generated
/// by the server and handed back in [TomFileMetadata.key]; a client that could
/// name its own key would turn any upload endpoint into an open write surface
/// on the blob store. The slot is what the application already controls through
/// its own authorization rules.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// final client = TomHttpFileTransferClient(
///   baseUri: Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/files'),
/// );
/// final cancellation = TomTransferCancellation();
///
/// final stored = await client.upload(
///   slot: 'invoices/4711/scan',
///   source: TomFileSource.bytes('scan.pdf', pdfBytes),
///   onProgress: (p) => print('${(p.fraction ?? 0) * 100}%'),
///   cancellation: cancellation,
/// );
///
/// await for (final chunk in client.download('invoices/4711/scan')) {
///   sink.add(chunk);
/// }
/// ```

classes.documentation:
/// Exception thrown when a file transfer fails.
///
/// Error keys are stable and worth matching on. Each names a condition a
/// running system can meet — a caller may branch on one, and `tom_flutter_ui`
/// turns each into a user-facing line — so a mistake in the calling code is
/// deliberately not among them:
///
/// - `file_transfer.not_found` — no file is stored in the requested slot.
/// - `file_transfer.cancelled` — the caller cancelled the transfer.
/// - `file_transfer.rejected` — the server refused the transfer (status code in
///   `parameters['statusCode']`).
/// - `file_transfer.invalid_descriptor` — the server's description of a stored
///   file was unreadable: not valid JSON, not a JSON object, missing `key` /
///   `mediaType` / `byteCount`, or a `HEAD` that described nothing at all.
/// - `file_transfer.invalid_slot` — the slot was empty or contained `.` or `..`
///   segments (see [TomHttpFileTransferClient.uriForSlot]).

/// The bytes to upload, together with what the server needs to know about them.
///
/// ## Why [open] is a function and not a stream
///
/// A Dart stream can be listened to once. Retry-after-failure — which the whole
/// progress/cancel UX exists to offer — needs the same bytes a second time, so
/// the source hands out a *factory*: every call to [open] starts a fresh read.
/// A source built from an in-memory buffer replays trivially; one built from a
/// file replays by re-opening the file.
///
/// ## Example
///
/// ```dart
/// // Small file, already in memory.
/// final small = TomFileSource.bytes('note.txt', utf8.encode('hello'));
///
/// // Large file, streamed from disk and re-openable.
/// final large = TomFileSource.stream(
///   fileName: 'video.mp4',
///   byteCount: await file.length(),
///   open: () => file.openRead(),
/// );
/// ```

